// HQ_8 / NQ_8: no MCQs for PSC (Part 2 only). OQ_8: open questions for PSC lectures p1-p13.
const HQ_8=[];
const NQ_8=[];
const OQ_8=[];

// ===== p1 Perspectives on sustainability (Næss 2001) =====
OQ_8.push(
{t:"p1",fmt:"essay",pts:25,
q:"(a) Comprehension: explain the difference between weak and strong sustainability. (b) Essay: can efficiency gains (greener buildings, a more compact city) deliver sustainable urban development on their own, or is sufficiency needed as well? Draw on Næss (2001), Huber's triad and at least one other course author or case.",
model:"(a) Weak sustainability comes from neo-classical economics in the 1970s (Solow, Hartwick) and assumes that natural capital and other capitals (financial, built, human) are substitutable: as long as total capital does not fall, using up nature can be compensated. The lecture's example is Oslo, ranked one of the most sustainable cities while financed by Norway's oil fund, natural capital converted into financial capital. Strong sustainability holds that natural capital is complementary, not interchangeable: there are biophysical limits that money or technology cannot buy away, the logic behind planetary boundaries.\n(b) Huber's triad (via Rudolf and Schmidt 2025) distinguishes efficiency (less impact per unit), consistency (qualitative change in industrial metabolism that mimics natural cycles) and sufficiency ('seeking enough when more is possible', Princen). Most urban sustainability policy is efficiency and consistency: energy-efficient buildings, compact form, electric vehicles. Næss accepts the compact city as the most energy-conscious urban form (Oslo residents further from downtown travel more by car) but argues it is not enough. Even if every new building is greener, the total load rises as long as the building stock keeps growing; new green buildings are only favourable in a relative sense unless they replace worse ones. His Nordic numbers make the point: Norwegians and Danes have more than 50 m2 of residential floor space each, twice as much as 30 to 35 years earlier. Hence his call for per-capita degrowth of the building stock in wealthy countries: a sufficiency strategy, grounded in Brundtland's global equity.\nOther course material supports this. Campbell notes that technology reduces resource use per unit but gains are often cancelled by rising demand (car fuel efficiency versus total gasoline use). Durrant et al. (L12) call this the Jevons paradox and see little evidence of absolute decoupling, so they propose ceilings and maximum space standards, which are sufficiency tools. Daly's distinction between growth (getting bigger) and development (getting better) points the same way.\nMy position: efficiency is necessary but not sufficient. It is politically easy because it fits a pro-growth agenda, which is also why it is attractive to the incumbents Swyngedouw warns about. Without sufficiency measures (limits on floor space, reuse of existing stock), efficiency gains are eaten up by growth, so cities can look sustainable per unit while total impacts rise.",
rubric:["(a) Correctly states substitutability (weak) vs complementarity/limits (strong), with origin or example","Explains efficiency, consistency and sufficiency (Huber) accurately","Uses Næss's argument on growth of the building stock / per-capita floor space and relative vs absolute improvement","Brings in at least one further author or case (Campbell, Durrant/Jevons, Daly, Oslo/Norway) correctly","Takes and justifies a clear position"]},

{t:"p1",fmt:"essay",pts:25,
q:"(a) Comprehension: what does Næss mean by 'ecology within the city' versus the 'city in ecology'? (b) Essay: should planning for sustainability be led by substantive goals or by good procedures (participation, consensus)? Discuss with Næss and at least two other course authors.",
model:"(a) Næss notes that sustainability criteria can conflict because they reflect two focal points. 'Ecology within the city' concerns the local environment of residents: parks, clean air and water, health. The 'city in ecology' sees the city as part of larger ecosystems, with ecological footprints and consequences far beyond its hinterland (energy use, emissions, land take elsewhere). Brundtland, he argues, requires planners to add the second perspective to the first.\n(b) Næss's core complaint is that procedural planning theory had become detached from substance. For sustainability that is inappropriate, because the right procedure depends on the goal: bottom-up planning works if local lifestyles fit the goals, not if those lifestyles are the problem. Sustainability means redistributing consumption 'from us who live here and now to those who live there and then', which will not emerge spontaneously from consensus in rich communities; local communities also face a prisoner's dilemma. He adds a knowledge argument (a committee agreeing that car traffic is harmless does not make it true) and a power argument (actors who profit from unsustainable development will not join a consensus). His answer: goal-oriented planning, scenarios and alliance-building; 'aiming to achieve consensus among all stakeholders is futile'.\nThe lecture's Neuman slide puts the opposite case: sustainable urban development implies 'the primacy of process over form', and liveability exists in compact and low-density cities alike. But the lecture also notes that a purely procedural view neglects substantive content. Campbell sits in between: the sustainable city emerges at the end of negotiated conflicts, not as their premise, yet procedure fails with all-or-nothing disputes and large power imbalances; his Coachella lizard and New Jersey plans were praised processes with doubtful outcomes. De Satgé and Watson (L6) add that consensus assumes a shared public interest, while in Southern cities state and residents hold conflicting rationalities.\nMy view: substance must lead, because process alone reproduces existing power and lifestyles, but substance needs procedure for legitimacy. Næss himself wants deliberation and voting combined, expert and lay knowledge combined, and planners who 'contain the strong and empower the weak'. The real question is not process versus goals but who sets the goals: Næss's alliances risk excluding opponents, which is why legitimacy (transparency, monitoring, democratic votes) remains essential.",
rubric:["(a) Correct explanation of both focal points and why Brundtland adds the 'city in ecology'","Explains Næss's critique of procedural/consensus planning with at least two of his arguments (goal-dependence, knowledge, power, prisoner's dilemma)","Engages a counter-position (Neuman's process over form, or communicative planning) fairly","Connects at least one other author (Campbell, de Satgé & Watson, Durrant) accurately","Reaches a nuanced, argued conclusion (e.g. who sets goals, legitimacy)"]},

{t:"p1",fmt:"essay",pts:25,
q:"(a) Comprehension: describe Vallance et al.'s (2011) three types of social sustainability. (b) Essay: is the social dimension the neglected pillar of urban sustainability? Use lecture material and cases from at least two lectures.",
model:"(a) Vallance et al. distinguish development sustainability (addressing basic needs, justice and equity), bridge sustainability (changes in social behaviour needed to reach bio-physical, environmental goals) and maintenance sustainability (preserving socio-cultural characteristics, what people want to keep). The lecture stresses that these can pull in different directions: bridge sustainability may require changing exactly what maintenance sustainability wants to preserve.\n(b) Lecture 1 calls social sustainability neglected and messy: there are many conceptions (liveability, justice, basic needs) that are hard to align, and it raises the most problematic questions, such as Global North versus South. Purvis et al. show that the familiar three-pillar or Venn diagram is a convention whose conceptual origins are unclear, so 'social' has often been an add-on rather than a worked-out goal.\nThe cases suggest neglect has consequences. Green gentrification (L3) is the clearest: greening, an environmental goal, raises land values and displaces the residents it should serve (Atlanta BeltLine; Anguelovski et al. 2022 find greening the lead explainer of gentrification in 8 of 17 cities). Campbell would call this the development conflict, the most elusive side of his triangle. Hansen et al. (L13) find that in eleven European 'champion' cities ecological themes grew strongly while social justice was the weakest lens: awareness rose but specific approaches were rare, a 'considerable disparity'. Angelo and Wachsmuth (L2) argue that dominant urban sustainability planning is socially narrow and focused on the liveability of wealthy residents.\nAt the same time 'neglect' is not the whole story. Brundtland's core is meeting everyone's basic needs, and Næss reads sustainable development as distributive ethics across generations and space. The problem is less that equity is absent from the concept than that it gets lost when sustainability is operationalised through measurable environmental targets and growth-friendly projects. Vallance et al.'s call for environmental scientists to work alongside social scientists on how residents interpret their places points to a remedy.\nConclusion: yes, in practice the social pillar is neglected, not because it is unimportant but because it is contested and hard to measure. Planners should treat it as a precondition (just green enough, anti-displacement measures, participation) rather than a side effect.",
rubric:["(a) Names and correctly defines development, bridge and maintenance sustainability","Uses lecture 1 points on why social sustainability is neglected/messy (or Purvis on pillars)","Uses at least two cases/authors from other lectures accurately (green gentrification cases, Campbell, Hansen, Angelo & Wachsmuth)","Shows nuance (e.g. Brundtland/Næss already contain equity; problem is operationalisation)","Clear conclusion that answers the question"]},

{t:"p1",fmt:"short",pts:5,
q:"Explain Swyngedouw's (2010) argument that sustainability is 'post-political', and why it matters for planners.",
model:"Swyngedouw argues that sustainability has become a consensus that it is 'impossible not to support'. It imagines an originally harmonious Nature that is now out of sync but can be restored through 'technological, managerial and organisational fixes'. Because everyone must endorse it, the concept closes off the genuinely political question of which natures we want to preserve, make or eradicate, and it can be captured by incumbent interests that may themselves perpetuate unsustainability. For planners this means that labelling a plan 'sustainable' says little: they should make the underlying choices and conflicts explicit (who gains, which nature) instead of hiding them behind a consensus term, as Næss also warns about a concept being 'watered out'.",
rubric:["Explains 'impossible not to support' / consensus that depoliticises","Mentions the question of which natures we want","Mentions capture by incumbents / technical fixes","Draws a planning implication"]},

{t:"p1",fmt:"apply",pts:10,
q:"A hypothetical Dutch municipality announces it will meet its climate goals by building 5,000 energy-neutral, spacious family homes on greenfield land at the edge of town, with a new road link. Assess this plan using Næss (2001).",
model:"Næss would judge the plan against his substantive criteria: cut energy use and emissions per person, minimise conversion of natural areas and farmland, and favour concentrated, land-saving structures. Three problems follow. First, location: greenfield edge development converts open land and, on his Oslo evidence, residents further from the centre travel more by car; the new road will attract more car commuting rather than relieve congestion. Second, the 'energy-neutral' label is a relative improvement: compared with ordinary new homes these are better, but unless they replace worse buildings the total stock and its load still grow. Third, 'spacious' homes continue the growth in floor space per person that Næss says must stop in wealthy countries; efficiency gains are cancelled by more square metres. He would recommend densification and re-use inside existing boundaries, selective standard improvement for those who lack adequate housing rather than more space for the well-housed, and a scenario comparing this 'trend' plan with an environmental alternative (as in NAMIT) so that politicians and the public see the consequences. He would also expect resistance from those who profit, and so advise building alliances with groups that share sustainability and equity goals.",
rubric:["Applies compact city / location and car-travel argument","Identifies relative vs absolute improvement (stock growth)","Identifies per-capita floor space growth as the core problem","Proposes Næss-consistent alternatives (densification, scenarios, alliances, selective standard improvement)","Uses Næss's concepts accurately, not generic sustainability talk"]},

{t:"p1",fmt:"compare",pts:10,
q:"Compare Næss (2001) and Campbell (1996) on the place of economic growth in sustainable development and on how planners should handle conflict.",
model:"On growth: Campbell puts economic growth at one corner of his triangle, on the same level as environmental protection and social equity, with sustainable development at the centre as a balance of the three. Næss explicitly criticises this in a note: in Brundtland growth is only a possible means, and only if its content changes, not a pole of sustainability. Næss sticks to a narrow, Brundtland-based reading centred on basic needs and ecological limits, and argues that continuous growth of the building stock in rich countries is incompatible with sustainability. Campbell, by contrast, accepts that growth can help ('trickle-down environmentalism') if it is fairly distributed.\nOn conflict: both take conflict seriously and reject a harmonious view. Campbell's answer is conflict resolution and negotiation, step by step towards an unreachable centre, with planners choosing between mediator and advocate roles. Næss is more partisan: consensus among all stakeholders is 'futile', so planners should build alliances among those who share basic equity and environmental values and confront those who profit from the status quo. In short, Campbell manages a plural balance; Næss takes the side of substantive limits.",
rubric:["Campbell: growth as one corner; balance at the centre","Næss: growth only a means, rejects its pole status (critique of Campbell)","Campbell: negotiation/conflict resolution, mediator or advocate","Næss: alliance-building, consensus futile","Clear point-by-point contrast rather than two separate summaries"]}
);

// ===== p2 Are cities sustainable? (Angelo & Wachsmuth 2020) =====
OQ_8.push(
{t:"p2",fmt:"essay",pts:25,
q:"(a) Comprehension: what do Angelo and Wachsmuth mean when they say the 'city as solution' view has been naturalised, and what is 'methodological cityism'? (b) Essay: what is gained and what is lost when sustainability is framed at the scale of the city? Use at least three authors or cases from the course.",
model:"(a) Naturalised means treated as obvious and inevitable. Angelo and Wachsmuth show that the shift 'from the city as a sustainability problem to the city as a sustainability solution' has a specific history (reactions to sprawl, informal settlements and climate change), so it 'was neither necessary nor inevitable': the sustainability conversation was not always urban, it became urban. Methodological cityism (their 2015 critique) is treating the city as the self-evident scale of analysis and action, looking only inside city boundaries.\n(b) Gains. The city is where much can be done: urban form shapes travel and energy use (Næss's Oslo gradient; compact cities with integrated transport and efficient buildings, as UN-Habitat argues). Cities can act when national governments stall: mayors signed the Chicago Climate Charter after the US withdrew from Paris, and networks such as C40 and ICLEI spread practice. Hodson et al. (L8) add that buses, light rail and cycling 'correspond' strongly to the urban scale, so cities have real leverage there.\nLosses. First, impacts leak beyond city limits. Næss's 'city in ecology' and A&W's multi-spatial agenda (operational landscapes of energy, extraction, logistics and waste) show that a city's footprint lies elsewhere. Krähmer's critique of Copenhagen and Durrant et al.'s point that 'beacon' cities look worse once consumption emissions are counted show how territorial accounting flatters wealthy cities. Second, politics narrows: A&W argue dominant urban sustainability is system-affirmative, pro-growth and tied to entrepreneurial competition, focused on wealthy residents' liveability (Austin's green clean-up shifted costs onto homeless people). Third, cities do not control everything: Hodson et al. stress that cities are 'not bounded containers' and that national states and global flows set conditions (UK bus deregulation).\nConclusion: the city is a useful lever but a misleading boundary. Planners should use urban tools while accounting for non-local impacts and asking who benefits, which is exactly the scrutiny A&W ask for instead of acceptance at face value.",
rubric:["(a) Correct meaning of naturalised (historically produced, not inevitable) and methodological cityism","Identifies real gains of the urban scale with evidence (urban form, mayors/networks, correspondence)","Identifies losses: non-local impacts/consumption emissions, political narrowing, multi-level constraints","Uses at least three course authors/cases accurately","Balanced conclusion"]},

{t:"p2",fmt:"essay",pts:25,
q:"(a) Comprehension: why is urban sustainability so difficult to measure? (b) Essay: can indicators and city rankings tell us which cities are sustainable? Draw on lecture 2 and at least two other lectures.",
model:"(a) Definitions vary widely: Mega and Pederson stress balancing progress through citizen participation, Hamilton et al. a built environment that meets needs without unacceptable impacts, Wu an adaptive process linking ecosystem services and well-being. Tanguay et al. (17 cities) find three problems: broad, vague definitions; no standard classification of sustainable development indicators; and data constraints. Data are thin: the World Bank has consistently measured CO2 estimates for only about 80 cities, and most rely on engineering parameters applied to survey data rather than actual emissions.\n(b) Indicators are useful: cities now report on the SDGs through Voluntary Local Reviews (Amsterdam, Utrecht), and indicators such as Amsterdam's modal split make progress visible. But rankings mislead in several ways. They usually count territorial impacts, while Næss's 'city in ecology' and A&W's multi-spatial analysis show that much of a city's footprint lies elsewhere. The Dutch footprint figures on the lecture 1 slides illustrate this at national scale: the land-use footprint is three times the country's land area. A city can therefore rank well while its residents' consumption is high, the 'beacon city' problem Durrant et al. cite from Krähmer. Weak sustainability compounds it: Oslo ranks among the most sustainable cities while financed by oil wealth. Who lives where matters too: Baiocchi et al. show low-carbon dense settlement types are dominated by specific social groups, so urban form and income go together.\nRankings also hide distribution. Bigger cities are richer but more unequal (UN DESA), and green gentrification (L3) shows that improving an environmental indicator can worsen equity. A&W add a representational point: images of model cities make policies look like common sense.\nConclusion: indicators tell us something about direction within a city, but they cannot settle whether a city is sustainable in an absolute sense. That would require consumption-based and justice indicators, and the lecture's open question remains: is absolute sustainability impossible, and do we need a relative conception?",
rubric:["(a) At least two of: varied definitions, Tanguay's three problems, World Bank data gap","Acknowledges the value of indicators (VLRs/SDGs) before critiquing","Uses territorial vs consumption/non-local impacts argument with a course source","Uses at least one equity/distribution argument (inequality, green gentrification, who lives in dense areas)","Draws on at least two other lectures accurately and concludes"]},

{t:"p2",fmt:"essay",pts:25,
q:"(a) Comprehension: describe how urban issues moved from the MDGs to the SDGs. (b) Essay: Angelo and Wachsmuth show that informal settlements were recast from environmental problem to model of sustainable urbanism. Should informal urbanism be a model for sustainable cities? Use at least two lectures.",
model:"(a) Under the MDGs, urban concerns were mainly a Southern slum issue: Goal 7 (environmental sustainability) included Target 7D, a significant improvement in the lives of at least 100 million slum-dwellers by 2020, and Target 7C on drinking water and sanitation. The SDGs shifted (i) from goals for the Global South to universal goals, (ii) to much more emphasis on social values and ecological limits, and (iii) to a standalone urban goal, SDG 11.\n(b) A&W trace how informal settlements, first framed as pollution and hazard problems (Habitat I, Brundtland's 'Third World Cities', The Challenge of Slums), were celebrated by 2010: UN-Habitat called urbanisation a positive force, Stewart Brand called slums 'unexpectedly green' because of density, walkability and recycling, Dharavi became a symbol of creativity and Aravena won the Pritzker Prize for incremental housing. The case for learning from informality is real: high density, low consumption, self-built incremental growth and community networks are what Northern compact-city planners try to engineer. Lecture 6 adds that residents in Maputo produce plans and maintain shared space themselves (inverse governmentality, self-production).\nBut there are strong reasons for caution. Lecture 6 notes that informal settlements are often on marginal, risky land with poor services; Roy calls the romantic image of the slum as 'vibrant and entrepreneurial urbanism' poverty pornography. Low consumption is often deprivation, not choice, which is Campbell's development conflict: environmental praise can become an excuse not to address poverty. De Satgé and Watson warn that framings travel without regard to context; a Northern celebration of 'green slums' can coexist with state programmes that aim to 'eradicate' informality (the N2 Gateway). Roy also shows informality is a state practice: the same informality is tolerated for wealthy malls and punished in poor settlements.\nConclusion: informal urbanism offers lessons (incrementalism, density, co-production), but making it a model risks aestheticising poverty. The better lesson is to support residents' own strategies with tenure security and services, not to treat their scarcity as a sustainability achievement.",
rubric:["(a) Target 7D/MDG Goal 7 and the three shifts to the SDGs (universal, social/ecological, SDG 11)","Accurately reconstructs A&W's problem-to-model shift with at least one example","Gives genuine arguments for learning from informality","Gives critical arguments from L6 (Roy, conflicting rationalities, risk/deprivation) or Campbell","Reasoned conclusion distinguishing lessons from romanticisation"]},

{t:"p2",fmt:"short",pts:5,
q:"Explain Angelo and Wachsmuth's pattern of moving from 'less city' to 'more city', and why climate change offered no obvious 'less city' option.",
model:"For each of their three roots, a problem first received a non-city solution and later a city solution. Sprawl was first answered by limiting growth (Limits to Growth, Callenbach's Ecotopia), later by compact and smart cities; informal settlements first by sustainable international development, later by treating them as models of resilient design; climate change first by multilateral treaties, later by interlocal networks and empowered mayors. Sprawl and slums could initially be met with 'less city', but climate change offered no such option: retreating from affluent coastal cities would mean an unthinkable loss of assets, and the 'urban age' ideology was already established. So cities became the dominant solution.",
rubric:["Explains non-city then city solution pattern","Gives at least two of the three roots with their solutions","Explains why climate change had no 'less city' option (assets, urban age ideology)"]},

{t:"p2",fmt:"apply",pts:10,
q:"A hypothetical Dutch city launches the campaign 'Climate-neutral by 2030', based only on emissions within municipal borders, and promotes a new dense, green waterfront district for high-income residents as its flagship. Analyse this using Angelo and Wachsmuth (2020) and the lecture.",
model:"Through A&W's research agenda the campaign raises four issues. Historically, it reproduces the now-dominant equation of sustainability with dense green urbanism, a consensus that became common sense rather than an inevitable truth. Multi-spatially, territorial accounting is a form of methodological cityism: it ignores the 'operational landscapes' of energy, materials and waste elsewhere, and the consumption emissions of residents; as the lecture's Copenhagen example shows (Krähmer), a carbon-neutral target can fail and green showcases can hide wider impacts. Politically, a flagship for high-income residents fits A&W's warning that urban sustainability is narrow and system-affirmative, focused on the liveability of wealthy residents whose consumption may cancel the savings of efficient districts; it may also displace lower-income households, a green gentrification risk (L3). Representationally, the slogan and images of the waterfront do work: they make a growth project look like climate policy and help the city compete with others (entrepreneurial governance). A&W would not reject the district, but would ask the city to report consumption-based impacts, show who benefits, and justify why this, rather than retrofits in existing neighbourhoods, is the priority.",
rubric:["Uses at least three of A&W's four research dimensions correctly (historical, multi-spatial, political, representational)","Identifies territorial vs consumption-based accounting problem","Identifies equity/who benefits issue (wealthy residents, gentrification)","Uses lecture example (Copenhagen/Krähmer) or entrepreneurial competition correctly","Gives concrete recommendations consistent with A&W (scrutiny, not rejection)"]},

{t:"p2",fmt:"compare",pts:10,
q:"Compare how Næss (2001) and Angelo and Wachsmuth (2020) treat the compact city.",
model:"Næss argues for the compact city as a substantive planning goal. Weighing urban models against his five criteria, he favours concentrated, land-saving structures: Oslo data show inner-city residents travel much less by car even after controlling for socio-economic factors, apartments need less heating per square metre, and densification converts less nature and farmland. He rejects the dispersed 'green city' and eco-villages. But he adds that compactness is not enough without sufficiency, since growth in floor space per person cancels efficiency gains.\nAngelo and Wachsmuth do not argue for or against compactness. They historicise it: the idea that density is green was counterintuitive until the 1980s to 2000s (Calthorpe 1985, Jenks et al., Owen 2004) and became the basis of climate plans worldwide as a reaction to sprawl. Their concern is that it became 'naturalised' and tied to entrepreneurial growth, and that wealthy downtown residents' consumption may cancel density's carbon savings.\nSo Næss is one of the voices that built the compact-city case, while A&W explain how that case became common sense. They meet on one point: density alone does not guarantee sustainability once consumption is counted.",
rubric:["Næss: compact city as goal with evidence (Oslo travel, heating, land)","Næss: compactness insufficient without sufficiency","A&W: historicise/critique the compact city as naturalised common sense, not a verdict on its merits","Identifies the shared point on consumption","Explicit comparison"]}
);

// ===== p3 Sustainability: a planning dilemma (Campbell 1996) =====
OQ_8.push(
{t:"p3",fmt:"essay",pts:25,
q:"(a) Comprehension: why does Campbell call the development conflict 'the most elusive' side of the planner's triangle? (b) Essay: Campbell writes that 'the development conflict is where the real action for planners will be'. Discuss this claim using cases and authors from at least two lectures.",
model:"(a) The development conflict sets social equity against environmental protection. It is elusive because it is not simply business versus nature or capital versus labour: it asks how the poor can gain opportunity if environmental protection slows growth. Globally, rich nations ask poor nations to forgo rapid development; locally, loggers and miners see environmentalists as elitists, and poor communities face a no-win choice between jobs and noxious land uses (incinerators, landfills) that richer areas can refuse, which puts environmental racism at its heart. Krumholz in Cleveland had to choose between regional rail (pollution reduction) and local buses (access for the inner-city poor).\n(b) The claim holds up well across the course. Green gentrification (L3) is a development conflict par excellence: greening the city raises land values and displaces those it should serve, as with the Atlanta BeltLine (home values rose 17.9 to 26.6% more within half a mile) or New York's greenways (Checker's environmental gentrification). Barcelona shows it is a planning choice: gentrification depended on location and existing market pressure. Witte et al. (L4) find the same at the port-city waterfront: climate-adaptive green redevelopment raises costs in historically blue-collar districts. Hansen et al. (L13) show European cities expanding ecological policies while social justice stays the weakest lens. Globally, de Satgé and Watson (L6) show how environmental and modernising goals ('eradicate' informal settlements) can hit the poorest.\nCampbell's own argument explains why this is where planners matter: the resource conflict can partly be handled by markets and regulation, the property conflict has a long tradition of equity planning, but the development conflict falls between environmental and community planners. His solution is collaboration between them (inner-city environmental health, joining the environmental justice movement) and solving it together with the property conflict, since only fairly distributed growth lets the poor protect their environment.\nA critique: post-growth authors (Durrant et al., L12) argue that framing sustainability as trade-offs has let planners avoid the deeper conflicts over resource use; the development conflict may be unsolvable without limits on the consumption of the rich (Næss). Still, Campbell is right that the credibility of sustainability planning now depends on whether environmental gains are distributed fairly; responses such as 'just green enough' and anti-displacement planning are attempts to do exactly that.",
rubric:["(a) Defines development conflict as equity vs environment and explains elusiveness with an example (noxious land uses, Krumholz, North-South)","Supports the claim with at least two cases from other lectures (green gentrification cases, port waterfront, Hansen, N2 Gateway)","Explains Campbell's own reasoning (collaboration, solving with property conflict)","Includes a critical perspective (post-growth/Næss) or limitation","Clear evaluative conclusion"]},

{t:"p3",fmt:"essay",pts:25,
q:"(a) Comprehension: describe the three perspectives on planning and urban sustainability presented in lecture 3 (normative, dilemma-based, critical) and the planner's role in each. (b) Essay: should planners act as mediators or as advocates in planning for sustainability? Use at least three course authors.",
model:"(a) The normative perspective asks what a sustainable city should look like (e.g. McPhearson et al.'s five principles, EEA, UN-Habitat); sustainability is a desirable condition and the planner is a goal setter and implementer using targets, indicators and plans. The dilemma-based perspective (Campbell's triangle) asks how competing objectives can be reconciled; sustainability is a balance and the planner a mediator and negotiator who identifies trade-offs and builds compromises. The critical perspective (Krähmer, Næss, post-growth) asks why cities keep producing unsustainability; sustainability is a contested political and socio-economic condition and the planner a critic or transformative actor who questions growth, power and consumption.\n(b) Campbell says planners cannot be at the centre of the triangle all the time; each must decide whether to be a mediator outside the conflict or an advocate within it, and both roles are needed. Mediation works when interests are compatible underneath, but he admits it fails with all-or-nothing disputes, large power imbalances or parties that refuse to take part.\nThe critical authors push towards advocacy. Næss argues that consensus among all stakeholders is futile because some actors profit from unsustainability; planners should build alliances and present sustainable alternatives and scenarios. Durrant et al. (L12) want planners to 'voice uncomfortable truths' about the infeasibility of growth-dependent plans, but ground this in expertise rather than personal values because planners are employees of elected politicians. De Satgé and Watson (L6) show why neutral mediation can mislead in Southern cities: the conflict is between rationalities, and a 'public interest' can be used to justify evictions (Delhi). Li and Dang (L11) add a new risk: if algorithms take over judgement, planners may become neither mediators nor advocates.\nMy view: the choice depends on the type of conflict. Where interests are negotiable (design of a park), mediation is appropriate. Where power is unequal or limits are non-negotiable (displacement, absolute ecological limits), mediation tends to reproduce the status quo and planners need an advocacy role, legitimised through transparent evidence about consequences (Næss's impact analysis, Durrant's expertise) rather than private conviction.",
rubric:["(a) All three perspectives with central idea and planner role","Accurately presents Campbell's mediator/advocate distinction and limits of negotiation","Uses at least two further authors (Næss, Durrant, de Satgé & Watson, Li & Dang) accurately","Conditions the answer on type of conflict/power","Addresses legitimacy of advocacy"]},

{t:"p3",fmt:"essay",pts:25,
q:"(a) Comprehension: what does Campbell mean by 'trickle-down environmentalism', and under which condition does he accept it? (b) Essay: can economic growth pay for environmental protection and social equity in cities? Discuss with authors and cases from at least three lectures.",
model:"(a) Campbell uses the term for the argument that growth makes environmental protection easier: grow the economy so society can afford to protect nature, an idea he sees in the Brundtland Report's call to revitalise global economic growth. He accepts part of it, but only if growth is fairly distributed: only then can the poor protect their environment, so the development conflict can be resolved only together with the property conflict.\n(b) There is evidence that growth funds greening. Hansen et al. (L13) find densification brought resources for green space, and Aarhus used 'exchange agreements' in which developers fund new green spaces in return for their gain. Dutch active land policy (L4) used land sales to finance public facilities, and the lecture's optimistic view of cities stresses agglomeration and innovation.\nBut the course raises three objections. First, distribution: green gentrification (L3) shows that growth-funded greening can concentrate benefits among those who can pay (Gould and Lewis's 'green growth machine': rising property values are both the signal of success and the predictor of displacement). Second, ecology: Daly separates growth (getting bigger) from development (getting better), and Durrant et al. (L12) find little evidence of absolute decoupling because of rebound (Jevons paradox) and problem shifting; that is why they exclude land value capture, which depends on creating ever more monetised development value. Næss (L1) shows efficiency gains cancelled by growth in floor space. Third, dependence: Dutch active land policy works only in rising markets; municipalities made losses in 2008 to 2010, so public goods tied to growth are fragile. China's land finance (L7) shows the extreme: local revenue from land sales drove sprawl with environmental and social costs.\nConclusion: growth can pay for some protection and equity, but making them dependent on growth ties public goals to the very process that creates pressure on land and resources. Campbell's condition (fair distribution) is rarely met, so planners should seek funding and instruments that do not require ever more development, as post-growth planning proposes.",
rubric:["(a) Correct definition with the Brundtland link and the fair-distribution condition","Gives evidence/arguments that growth can fund greening (Aarhus, land policy, agglomeration)","Distribution objection with green gentrification/growth machine","Ecological objection with decoupling/Jevons/Daly/Næss","Growth-dependence objection (active land policy 2008-10, China land finance) and a conclusion"]},

{t:"p3",fmt:"short",pts:5,
q:"Explain the difference between Type I, II and III problems (DeFries & Nagendra 2017) and why the distinction matters for urban sustainability planning.",
model:"Type I problems are tame: definable and solvable with known methods. Type II problems can be defined, but there is no clear solution. Type III problems are neither clearly definable nor solvable; they require continual learning and adaptation, which is the wicked problem of Rittel and Webber (and Ackoff's 'mess' of interrelated problems). Urban sustainability is largely Type III: many stakeholders, many interpretations and uncertain futures. That is why lecture 3 speaks of a 'dilemmas paradigm': planners should not look for perfect solutions but manage trade-offs and conflicts, seeking a viable compromise, and treat plans as learning processes rather than blueprints.",
rubric:["Correctly defines Type I, II and III","Links Type III to wicked problems/messes","Draws the planning implication (no perfect solutions, learning, trade-offs)"]},

{t:"p3",fmt:"apply",pts:10,
q:"A hypothetical Dutch regional authority has budget for one transport project: a fast rail link that lures suburban commuters out of their cars, or a dense, frequent bus network serving low-income neighbourhoods with poor job access. Analyse the choice with Campbell's planner's triangle.",
model:"This mirrors Krumholz's dilemma in Cleveland, which Campbell uses to illustrate the development conflict (equity versus environment). The rail link serves the green corner: it reduces car use and emissions, and also supports the growth corner by strengthening the regional labour market. The bus network serves the equity corner: access to jobs and services for those without cars. Choosing rail risks an 'elite environmentalism' in which the main beneficiaries are better-off suburban commuters; choosing buses may deliver smaller emission cuts. Campbell would not expect the planner to reach the centre in one step. Procedurally, he would advise negotiation to find complementary interests, for example phasing, or designing the rail link with feeder buses and fares that serve low-income areas, and translation between the languages of environmentalists, economists and equity advocates. He warns that negotiation fails with all-or-nothing choices and power imbalances, which applies here because the budget allows only one project. Substantively, land-use planning matters: concentrating housing and jobs around stations could make one investment serve both goals. Finally the planner must decide whether to mediate or to advocate; Campbell suggests the development conflict is where planners should engage most actively.",
rubric:["Identifies the development conflict and maps options onto triangle corners","Recognises the Krumholz analogy or elite environmentalism","Applies Campbell's procedural path and its limits (all-or-nothing, power)","Applies a substantive path (land use, combined solutions)","Addresses the planner's mediator/advocate choice"]},

{t:"p3",fmt:"compare",pts:10,
q:"Compare how Campbell (1996) and de Satgé and Watson (2018) understand conflict in planning.",
model:"Campbell sees conflict as structural but negotiable. The three sides of his triangle are real clashes of interest (property, resource and development conflicts), not misunderstandings, and they go to 'the historic core of planning'. Yet the corners also depend on each other, so planners can search for complementary interests through negotiation, conflict resolution and translation between the languages of economics, ecology and social justice; the sustainable city emerges incrementally from these negotiations.\nDe Satgé and Watson see a deeper conflict. 'Conflicting rationalities' are clashes of world views between techno-managerial, modernising, marketised state planning and marginalised communities surviving under informality. Power defines what counts as rational, and identity and language filter meaning. Consensus-seeking, Habermasian procedures cannot resolve this; their proposition is that a conflict of rationalities, not a shared 'public interest', is the starting point for planning in Southern cities.\nBoth reject harmony and the idea that good communication dissolves conflict, and Campbell too warns that the economic language can dominate like a colonial lingua franca. But Campbell assumes legitimate interests within a shared frame that can be traded off; de Satgé and Watson question that shared frame itself.",
rubric:["Campbell: three structural conflicts, but negotiable via conflict resolution/translation","De Satgé & Watson: conflicting rationalities as clashing world views, power defines rationality","Their critique of consensus/public interest","Identifies a shared point (rejecting harmony; lingua franca) and the key difference (shared frame)"]}
);

// ===== p4 The Dutch planning system (Witte, Wiegmans & Louw 2025) =====
OQ_8.push(
{t:"p4",fmt:"essay",pts:25,
q:"(a) Comprehension: explain what Witte et al. (2025) mean by 'more claims than land', distinguishing net from cumulative claims. (b) Essay: how can Dutch planning deal with competing land claims from the energy transition, housing, climate adaptation and the port economy? Use the reading, lectures 4 and 5 and at least one other lecture.",
model:"(a) In Rotterdam and Amsterdam the net port area has been fairly stable, so in net terms the claims do not yet outnumber the available land. But vacant land has fallen (Rotterdam from about 20% to 11%, Amsterdam from 18% to 8%), and new claims multiply: energy transition, circular economy, climate adaptation and water storage, housing, biodiversity, external safety and EU environmental rules. Cumulatively these exceed the land, so 'the battle for space is about to intensify'. Land can even be physically empty yet unusable: in Westpoort some plots could not be allocated because there was no noise capacity left.\n(b) Witte et al. propose to identify conflicts by analysing land supply over time and to mitigate them by distinguishing levels (port region, port-city interface, waterfront) and types of claims (urban, environmental, economic). The Houtrakpolder shows why levels matter: the province reserves it for port expansion, Haarlemmermeer contests this, and it is also considered for rainwater storage, while Haven-Stad housing costs about 135 ha of port sites. Such conflicts cannot be solved at the waterfront alone.\nThe Dutch system offers instruments (L4). Under the Omgevingswet, the Omgevingsvisie integrates interests, municipalities regulate through the Omgevingsplan, and provinces and the state can intervene with a project decision; complementary task allocation lets the most suitable level act. Land instruments include pre-emption, expropriation and leasehold: Amsterdam closed a coal terminal by not renewing its leasehold, freeing space for energy and circular uses. Much coordination happens 'alongside' the formal system through visions and covenants (polderen). But there are limits: port authorities are publicly owned companies that the council barely steers (grey accountability), covenants can be fragile (Maasvlakte II nature compensation lagged and nature organisations threatened court action), and Moerdijk (L5) shows that energy-transition claims can reopen the question whether a whole village stays.\nCampbell's triangle, which Witte et al. use, clarifies what is at stake: resource, property and development conflicts all appear at the port-city. Post-growth thinking (L12) would add that not every claim can be met; planners may need ceilings and phase-outs, as Amsterdam's fossil phase-out partly does.\nConclusion: the Dutch system can structure these conflicts through multi-level coordination and land instruments, but it cannot make the land bigger; prioritisation and democratic accountability for that prioritisation are the real challenge.",
rubric:["(a) Distinguishes stable net area from cumulative claims; uses evidence (vacant land, noise capacity)","Explains the multi-level/multi-type mitigation framework with a case (Houtrakpolder/Haven-Stad)","Uses Dutch instruments correctly (Omgevingswet instruments, project decision, leasehold, polderen)","Identifies limits (accountability of port authority, covenants, Moerdijk)","Links another lecture (Campbell, post-growth, China) and concludes"]},

{t:"p4",fmt:"essay",pts:25,
q:"(a) Comprehension: describe the three municipal intervention strategies in Dutch planning (inside, outside, alongside) with the promise and risk of each. (b) Essay: is the Dutch planning system equipped to deliver a 'just city'? Use lecture 4, the reading and at least one other lecture.",
model:"(a) Inside: the municipality as public stakeholder uses public land instruments (pre-emption, expropriation, leasehold, the Omgevingsplan); promise: legal certainty and legitimacy; risk: rigidity and lengthy procedures. Outside: the municipality as private stakeholder through active land policy (buy, service and sell land, as in VINEX); promise: control over development and financing; risk: the 'double hat' (regulator and developer at once) and financial risk, since it only works in rising markets (losses in 2008 to 2010; Rijnenburg again). Alongside: informal, open planning or polderen, involving stakeholders early to build draagvlak, with law codifying agreements later; promise: co-creation and conflict reduction; risk: tokenism and unequal power relations.\n(b) Strengths: the system has a constitutional basis (Art. 21: habitability and protection of the living environment), a strong legal framework with legal protection, and functions such as comprehensive weighing of claims and democratic justification. The Omgevingswet adds care for the living environment and, as Lin et al. (L10) note, emphasises digital participation.\nWeaknesses for justice: lecture 4 asks who owns the land, who is represented, and points to meta-governance, grey accountability and power imbalances. Witte et al. show port authorities are publicly owned companies that lease land but are not public decision-making bodies, so the council has little direct grip. Waterfront regeneration attracts young professionals and raises prices, pushing out port workers and original residents. Moerdijk (L5) shows compensation planning instead of preventing harm, and residents uncertain for years about whether their village stays. Informal planning can be co-creation or tokenism; Hansen et al. (L13) find participation in European green space planning mostly stays at information and consultation.\nComparison helps: China's authoritarian deliberation (L7) shows participation that is legalised but late and limited, while the Dutch system offers more formal rights. But Campbell's warning applies to both: nothing inherent in planning steers it towards justice.\nConclusion: the Dutch system is well equipped procedurally (legal protection, multi-level coordination), less so substantively. Justice depends on how instruments are used: whether informal processes include weaker parties, whether land policy serves affordable housing rather than revenue, and whether bodies like port authorities can be held to account.",
rubric:["(a) All three strategies with position, promise and risk correct","Identifies systemic strengths (legal certainty, Art. 21, Omgevingswet)","Uses at least two justice concerns from L4/reading (grey accountability, tokenism, gentrification at waterfront, double hat)","Uses at least one other lecture accurately (Moerdijk, China, Hansen, Campbell)","Distinguishes procedural from substantive justice in the conclusion"]},

{t:"p4",fmt:"essay",pts:25,
q:"(a) Comprehension: explain the Dutch 'decentralised unitary state' and the roles and time horizons of the three government levels in planning. (b) Essay: compare how the Netherlands and China organise spatial planning to reconcile development and protection. Which system is better equipped for sustainability?",
model:"(a) The Netherlands combines a Napoleonic, top-down legal tradition with Thorbecke's (1848) decentralised unitary state: lower governments have partial autonomy within national frameworks ('House of Thorbecke' vertical coordination). The national level makes strategic choices with a long horizon (more than 10 years), provinces translate these into spatial visions at a tactical, medium-term level (5 to 10 years), and municipalities make legally binding land-use plans (now the Omgevingsplan) at an operational level (under 5 years). The Omgevingswet keeps this scheme but adds complementary task allocation: depending on the task, the most suitable level acts, provinces become stronger area managers, and province or state can intervene locally through a project decision.\n(b) China's system (L7, Liu and Zhou) was long fragmented: four main plan types under different ministries with planning periods of 15, 20, 10 and 5 years, different land classifications, frequent revisions and no superior law, which produced sprawl, farmland loss and ecological damage. Land finance after the 1994 fiscal reform pushed local governments towards growth. The 2018 to 2019 reform created one ministry (MNR), five levels, 'multiple plans in one', a 'One Map' and three control lines (ecological red line, permanent basic farmland, urban development boundary), backed by a planned law.\nThe Dutch system integrates through law and negotiation rather than through a single ministry: the Omgevingsvisie integrates interests, but much coordination happens informally (polderen) and extra-legally. Both systems face 'more claims than land', but handle it differently: China by drawing binding lines from above, the Netherlands by multi-level negotiation (Witte et al.).\nFor sustainability, China's control lines give hard limits on land take, something Næss would welcome, and it can act at scale; but implementation depends on central-local interests, legal backing came late (Guangzhou's regulations only in 2025), and participation is 'authoritarian deliberation'. The Dutch system offers legitimacy, legal protection and participation, but negotiation favours those with resources, and active land policy ties public goals to rising markets.\nConclusion: neither is simply better. China is stronger on protection by decree, the Netherlands on legitimacy and adaptability; sustainable planning needs both binding limits and democratic negotiation, which each system currently has in different measure.",
rubric:["(a) Decentralised unitary state explained; three levels with roles and horizons; Omgevingswet change","Accurate account of China's fragmentation and NTSP reform (MNR, control lines, One Map)","Contrasts integration mechanisms (top-down lines vs negotiation/polderen)","Weighs sustainability strengths and weaknesses of both, incl. participation","Reasoned conclusion"]},

{t:"p4",fmt:"short",pts:5,
q:"Explain the 'double hat' problem of Dutch active land policy and why it only works in rising markets.",
model:"In active land policy the municipality acts as a private stakeholder: it buys land, services it and sells building plots, often in public-private partnerships (VINEX, now NOVEX). It then wears two hats: it is both the public regulator that decides what may be built and a land developer with a financial interest in what is built. That can bias planning decisions towards revenue. The model relies on rising land prices, because the municipality pre-finances acquisition and servicing and recovers costs through later sales ('price follows the product'). When the market collapsed in 2008 to 2010, municipalities were left with expensive land and made heavy losses; the lecture notes the same story is repeating (Rijnenburg).",
rubric:["Defines active land policy (buy, service, sell)","Explains double hat: regulator and developer","Explains dependence on rising prices with 2008-10 losses"]},

{t:"p4",fmt:"apply",pts:10,
q:"A hypothetical Dutch city wants to transform an active inner-city port area into 3,000 homes, while the port authority wants room for hydrogen and circular industry. Analyse the situation with Witte et al.'s (2025) multi-facetted framework.",
model:"Multi-level: the conflict appears at the waterfront (homes next to remaining industry), at the port-city interface (port and city competing for the same space) and at the port-region level, because displaced port functions need land elsewhere in the region, where they may meet other claims (as with the Houtrakpolder). Multi-dimensional: economically, Charlier warns that 'residual maritime potential' is non-renewable: once port land becomes city, port functions never return. Environmentally, housing near industry consumes environmental space (noise, safety, emissions); Rotterdam's 230 housing plans inside the port's environmental zone and Westpoort's lack of noise capacity show that homes can block port use of nearby plots. Institutionally, zoning lies with the municipality, while the port authority is a publicly owned company leasing land, so coordination is needed. Socio-culturally, waterfront regeneration can gentrify a historically blue-collar area. Multi-actor: port businesses, municipality, port authority and local communities all have stakes. Mitigation: map land supply and claims over time, decide at which level each claim fits best, sequence transformation with relocation sites, and set environmental zoning conditions to protect residents, as the framework suggests.",
rubric:["Applies all three levels","Applies multiple dimensions incl. environmental space and residual maritime potential","Identifies the actors and the institutional position of port authority vs municipality","Proposes mitigation consistent with the framework","Uses reading evidence accurately"]},

{t:"p4",fmt:"compare",pts:10,
q:"Witte et al. (2025) build on Campbell's planner's triangle. Compare how the three conflicts appear in Campbell's original and in the port-city interface.",
model:"In Campbell, the resource conflict (economy versus environment) is business resisting yet needing regulation of nature, symbolised by city limits and examples like timber versus the spotted owl. Witte et al. locate it in ports as sites of production and transshipment: good for jobs and agglomeration, but with pollution, congestion and emissions, hence today's circularity debate and limits of 'environmental use space'. Campbell's property conflict (economy versus equity) concerns competing claims on property, landlords versus tenants, gentrifiers versus long-time residents, and the private sector both resisting and needing intervention. Witte et al. find it in the mix of public and private land ownership, which shapes whether port land can be redeveloped, and in waterfront regeneration that pushes out port workers and original residents. Campbell's development conflict (equity versus environment) is the most elusive, about the poor and environmental protection. In ports, just urban development must also be green, which raises costs in blue-collar districts; climate-adaptive redevelopment can increase gentrification. The difference: Campbell gives a general template and insists real conflicts need case studies; Witte et al. provide that case by spatialising the triangle across levels (region, interface, waterfront) and adding institutional and actor dimensions.",
rubric:["Resource conflict in both texts","Property conflict in both texts","Development conflict in both texts","Identifies how Witte et al. extend Campbell (levels, actors, case-specific)"]}
);

// ===== p5 Fight, flight or freeze? Governing Moerdijk (slides + Witte et al. 2025) =====
// Thinner material (no separate reading; slides only): 2 essays + 2 shorter questions.
OQ_8.push(
{t:"p5",fmt:"essay",pts:25,
q:"(a) Comprehension: explain the three governance approaches 'fight', 'flight' and 'freeze' for villages next to expanding ports or industry, with an example of each from lecture 5. (b) Essay: what would be a just outcome for the village of Moerdijk? Use lecture 5 and at least two other course authors.",
model:"(a) Fight: the village stays and must co-exist with the port; Pernis, Rozenburg and Heijplaat in Rotterdam, with Doel and Ruigoord marked with a question mark. Flight: the village as a whole goes, it is moved or removed; Kiruna in Sweden, where houses were literally moved, and Immerath (Garzweiler). Freeze: the village is not formally moved but left in limbo so it slowly disappears; Weiwerd, Oterdum and Heveskes near Delfzijl, Nieuwesluis and Blankenburg in Rotterdam, Antwerp villages and Lützerath.\n(b) Moerdijk is a village of about 1,100 residents next to a strategic port and industrial area. After the 2011 Chemie-Pack fire, planning shifted from strategy (Port Vision 2030) to compensation (the Moerdijk regulation). In 2025 the energy transition (PowerPort, around 700 ha needed in the region, a new 380 kV substation) brought back the plan to remove the village; the municipal council agreed in November 2025, but the province asked for more research, the decision was postponed and in May 2026 the municipality said the village might stay after all.\nA just outcome depends on whose claims count. Campbell's triangle shows a property conflict (port growth versus residents' homes) and a development conflict (energy transition versus local equity): a national environmental good imposes concentrated local costs. Compensation planning is a negotiated trade-off, but residents' demand for up to 350% compensation rather than 100%, and statements like 'Mother is buried here', show that money does not fully replace place. Næss would accept that a sustainability goal can override a minority, but he also warns against 'majority tyranny' over minorities' vital interests. De Satgé and Watson offer a third lens: the port's techno-economic rationality and villagers' attachment to place are conflicting rationalities meeting at an interface, so the question whether this is 'just NIMBYism' itself depends on which rationality defines the problem.\nProcedural justice is weakest: messages kept changing, the state reportedly knew for months that the village had to go, and the lecturer himself called the plan shaky. Freeze, the worst outcome, arises precisely from indecision.\nMy view: a just outcome requires first a clear, timely decision by an accountable level, a real assessment of alternatives (can the substantive need be met elsewhere?), and, if relocation is unavoidable, residents' say in how and where (a collective relocation rather than a slow freeze), with compensation beyond market value.",
rubric:["(a) Correct definitions and correctly placed examples (freeze: Delfzijl villages; flight: Kiruna/Immerath; fight: Pernis etc.)","Accurate case facts (Chemie-Pack, compensation planning, PowerPort, 2025-26 decision process)","Uses at least two authors accurately (Campbell, Næss, de Satgé & Watson, Witte et al.)","Distinguishes substantive and procedural justice","Argued conclusion"]},

{t:"p5",fmt:"essay",pts:25,
q:"(a) Comprehension: explain the shift in Moerdijk from strategic planning ('Port Vision 2030') to compensation planning (the 'Moerdijk regulation'). (b) Essay: who should decide about the future of a village like Moerdijk, and with which instruments? Use lectures 4 and 5 and at least one other lecture.",
model:"(a) Strategic planning sets a long-term vision for how the port area should develop (Port Vision 2030, after the 2013 port development strategy). Compensation planning accepts that the port will develop and arranges measures to offset harm to residents instead of choosing where village and port should be: the Moerdijk regulation, with investments of 10 million plus 8 million euros following the Committee Nijpels. The response was remedial and financial rather than a spatial decision.\n(b) Several levels have stakes. The national government frames the energy transition and found in 2025 that about 700 ha were needed in the Powerport region; the province is the area manager under the Omgevingswet; the municipality writes the Omgevingsplan and its council agreed in November 2025 that the village should go; the port authority and companies want space; residents want certainty. Lecture 4 already named Moerdijk as an example of 'unclear strategies of different levels of government', and the timeline confirms it: the province asked for more research, the decision was postponed by half a year, a caretaker cabinet would not decide, and it emerged that the state had known for months.\nThe Dutch toolkit could settle the matter: a province or ministry can use a project decision; pre-emption rights and expropriation secure land; compensation can be arranged; and participation is foreseen (the national government spoke of 'a just and humane approach' with roles for residents, port authority, industry and farmers). The risk is the opposite of decisiveness: freeze, where no one decides yet the village declines.\nOther lectures sharpen the question. Witte et al. (L4) show that 'more claims than land' conflicts arise at several levels, so a purely municipal decision is too narrow when the need is national. Campbell (L3) warns that procedure fails with all-or-nothing choices and power imbalances, which fits a village that either stays or goes. China's top-down control lines (L7) show a decisive alternative, but at the cost of limited participation.\nMy view: because the 'greater good' is national (energy infrastructure), the national level should decide and bear the costs, using a project decision, but only after transparent comparison of alternative sites and real co-decision by residents on compensation and relocation. Accountability, not the choice of instrument, is the main gap in Moerdijk.",
rubric:["(a) Correctly contrasts strategic and compensation planning with Moerdijk regulation/Nijpels","Identifies relevant actors and levels with case facts","Uses Dutch instruments from L4 correctly (project decision, pre-emption, expropriation, Omgevingswet roles)","Identifies freeze/indecision and accountability problem","Links at least one other lecture and gives a justified position"]},

{t:"p5",fmt:"short",pts:5,
q:"Lecture 5 asks whether the Moerdijk case is 'a typical case of NIMBYism'. Explain what NIMBY means and why the label may not fit.",
model:"NIMBY ('not in my back yard') describes residents who accept a facility in principle but oppose it near their homes, implying a narrow self-interest against the wider public good. In Moerdijk the label is doubtful. Residents are not resisting a nuisance next door but the possible disappearance of their whole village, including homes, community and history ('Save our village, preserve our history'). The burden is extremely concentrated: a national 'greater good' (energy transition, port growth) imposes the entire local cost on about 1,100 people. And the process itself was unclear, with changing messages from different levels of government. Calling this NIMBYism frames a justice and governance question as mere self-interest.",
rubric:["Defines NIMBY","Explains why stakes exceed a nuisance (loss of village/place)","Mentions concentrated costs vs greater good or the governance problem"]},

{t:"p5",fmt:"apply",pts:10,
q:"Apply Witte et al.'s (2025) multi-facetted framework (levels, dimensions, actors) to the Moerdijk case.",
model:"Levels: at the port-region level, port regionalisation and the national search for about 700 ha in the Powerport region (Geertruidenberg, Drimmelen, Moerdijk) for energy infrastructure, cable routes and industrial growth; at the port-city interface, the village directly next to the port and industrial area; at the local edge, buffer zones and nuisance (residents complained about noise when trees in a green buffer in Klundert were cut). Dimensions: economic (port and industrial growth), environmental and safety (the 2011 Chemie-Pack fire, external risk, environmental contours), energy (a new 380 kV substation), institutional (municipality, province and state with unclear strategies) and socio-cultural (attachment to place, history, the church and churchyard). Actors: the port and companies, the municipality (zoning), the port authority, the province, the national government and residents, plus farmers whose land is crossed by cables. The framework shows why the conflict has escalated: claims at the regional level (energy transition) land on one village at the interface, while no single actor is clearly responsible. It also suggests mitigation: deal with the energy claim at the regional level where alternative sites can be compared, rather than only at the village edge.",
rubric:["Applies all three levels with case facts","Names several dimensions incl. safety and energy transition","Identifies the actors and the coordination problem","Draws a conclusion or mitigation from the framework"]}
);

// ===== p6 Planning for sustainability in African cities (de Satgé & Watson 2018) =====
OQ_8.push(
{t:"p6",fmt:"essay",pts:25,
q:"(a) Comprehension: explain Roy's view of informality as a 'mode of urbanisation' and a state practice. (b) Essay: should planners formalise informal settlements? Discuss the debate using lecture 6, de Satgé and Watson, and at least one other lecture.",
model:"(a) Roy rejects seeing informality as a sector or a temporary deviation that development will remove. It is a mode of urbanisation: much of the city is produced outside formal rules. And it is a state practice: the state decides what counts as legitimate, so a wealthy mall or gated estate breaching rules can be 'informal' yet legitimate, while poor settlements are declared illegal. Informality is thus 'a heuristic device that serves to deconstruct the very basis of state legitimacy and its various instruments: maps, surveys, property, zoning and, most importantly, the law'.\n(b) The case for formalisation is strongest in De Soto: give informal dwellers legal title so these 'heroic entrepreneurs' can unlock capital, access credit and functioning property markets. Formal status can bring services and protection. Critics on the lecture slide answer that secure tenure does not depend on title: it comes from protection from eviction and from legitimation through services and infrastructure; people improve housing without titles; titling is costly for the poor; and formalisation often favours those able to exploit the housing market.\nDe Satgé and Watson show what formalisation can mean in practice. South Africa's Breaking New Ground policy (2004) promised in situ upgrading but also 'the focused eradication of informal settlements'; the N2 Gateway targeted Joe Slovo for eradication and replacement, escalating conflicts between borners, backyarders, hostel dwellers and informal settlers and leading to protests, eviction attempts and a Constitutional Court judgement. Formalisation was the state's techno-managerial rationality meeting residents' 'will to survive and thrive'. Lecture 6 also notes the shift from resettlement to in situ, participatory upgrading, but warns it can benefit landlords.\nOther lectures add perspective. Angelo and Wachsmuth (L2) show informal settlements recast as sustainable models, a framing that can romanticise deprivation. Shenzhen's urban villages (L7), housing a majority of residents, show informal housing absorbing migrants that formal systems (hukou) exclude.\nConclusion: formalisation as titling or eradication often harms those it claims to help. A better route is incremental tenure security plus services and co-produced upgrading that starts from residents' own practices (inverse governmentality, self-production in Maputo), with protection against displacement of tenants.",
rubric:["(a) Informality as mode of urbanisation and state practice (legitimisation), ideally with Roy's example or quote","De Soto's argument and the critics' points correctly","Uses de Satgé & Watson's N2 Gateway/BNG/Joe Slovo case accurately","Links at least one other lecture (A&W, Shenzhen urban villages, Campbell)","Clear position distinguishing titling/eradication from tenure security/upgrading"]},

{t:"p6",fmt:"essay",pts:25,
q:"(a) Comprehension: name three legacies of colonial planning in African cities and explain one in depth. (b) Essay: de Satgé and Watson argue that power defines what counts as rational. Discuss how planning knowledge presented as 'technical' can exclude people, using examples from at least three lectures.",
model:"(a) Legacies include fragmentation of infrastructure, elite capture of former European areas, evictions and commodification of land, dual land laws, institutionalised racism, and planning assumptions (tax bases, data capacity) that did not hold. Fragmentation in depth: colonial planning built services for the colonial city only. In Maputo the public water network still serves mainly the old city, while the peri-urban districts depend on small private piped systems run by independent providers; poorer or segregated populations also live further from economic centres, creating mobility burdens.\n(b) Following Foucault and Flyvbjerg and Richardson, de Satgé and Watson argue that 'power is used (by actors in a particular context) to define what is counted as rationality and so what is counted as reality'. Framing knowledge as technical or expert is itself an exercise of power, and the 'interface' (Li) is where issues are 'rendered technical'.\nExamples from the course. Colonial planning (L6) used 'public health' discourses and green belts to exclude, and apartheid planners used Northern best practices (Garden Cities, neighbourhood units, Radburn) to justify forced removals; Langa itself was a 1923 Garden City township. In Delhi, courts used the planning norm of acting 'in the public interest' to justify evictions (Bhan). On the N2 Gateway, officials were discouraged from engagement because a democratic government 'knows what the people want'. Language is also a filter: English dominates policy, so officials who do not speak Xhosa cannot enter residents' worlds unguided.\nThe same mechanism appears elsewhere. Li and Dang (L11) warn that algorithmic 'optimal' outputs gain authority, displace practitioner judgement, and reproduce historic inequities such as redlining while ignoring values that cannot be quantified. Lin et al. (L10) cite Sydney, where political factors shaped every part of urban analytics, from which data were available to how they were used. Campbell (L3) warns that the economic language can dominate like a colonial lingua franca.\nThis does not make expertise worthless; Næss (L1) insists ecological facts are not settled by agreement. The lesson is that technical knowledge must be made accountable: whose data, which categories, who is absent. Planners should catalogue the rationalities in a place before intervening and combine expert and local knowledge.",
rubric:["(a) Three correct legacies; one explained in depth with an example (e.g. Maputo water)","Explains rationality-as-power with the Foucault/Flyvbjerg basis and the interface","Uses at least two L6 examples accurately (Garden City/apartheid, Delhi public interest, N2 Gateway, language)","Extends to at least one other lecture (AI bias, Sydney analytics, Campbell lingua franca)","Nuanced conclusion (expertise vs accountability)"]},

{t:"p6",fmt:"essay",pts:25,
q:"(a) Comprehension: what is the 'southern theorising project', and why is 'southern' a perspective rather than a geography? (b) Essay: are new cities, gated communities and transit corridors a route to sustainable urbanism in African cities? Use lecture 6 and at least two other lectures.",
model:"(a) De Satgé and Watson prefer a 'southern theorising project' to a finished southern theory. It is critical of existing, often Northern theory that claims universal validity; rooted in place (Healey's 'origin narratives', Flyvbjerg's phronetic case research); open to 'meso-level' generalisation between universalism and particularism; and it critically appropriates existing lenses rather than rejecting them. 'Southern' is relational and perspectival: seeing from the South exposes the parochialism of pseudo-universal theory, so it is 'a conceptual rather than a physical location', not a new North/South binary.\n(b) Lecture 6 presents three trends. Transit-oriented development, such as Johannesburg's 'Corridors of Freedom', aims to overcome apartheid inequality through compact growth corridors with bus rapid transit and mixed use. That echoes the compact-city logic Næss (L1) and UN-Habitat defend. Yet it was only partly realised because of lacking funding and changing political will. Gated communities and new cities (Tatu City in Kenya) are aimed at higher and middle incomes, often exclusionary, and financed by global property developers. Keeton and Nijhuis list their problems: poor public transport, little public space, limited housing diversity, unprotected ecological zones, sprawl and high resource consumption; they cause fragmentation, evictions and prices out of touch with context.\nThe comparison with China (L7) is useful: eco-cities such as Sino-Singapore Tianjin were built as showcases with imported technology, and the lecture asks whether they change the system or remain isolated. Angelo and Wachsmuth (L2) show how 'sustainable city' models travel globally and become common sense. De Satgé and Watson would treat such models with suspicion: they 'land' with little investigation of fit and often reproduce the exclusions of earlier imported models, as Garden Cities did under apartheid.\nConclusion: new cities and gated estates mostly undermine sustainability by fragmenting cities and serving elites; TOD is more promising in principle but depends on sustained public capacity, which is exactly what Southern contexts often lack. Sustainable urbanism here must start from how most residents actually live, largely informally (85.8% of jobs in Africa are informal), not from showcase projects.",
rubric:["(a) Southern theorising project features and the perspective-not-geography point","Accurately describes TOD/Corridors of Freedom and its partial realisation","Accurately describes new cities/gated communities and Keeton & Nijhuis challenges","Links at least two other lectures (Næss/compact city, China eco-cities, A&W travelling models)","Conclusion grounded in Southern context (informality, capacity)"]},

{t:"p6",fmt:"short",pts:5,
q:"Explain what de Satgé and Watson (after Li 2007) mean by the 'interface', and why it matters for planners.",
model:"The interface is the times and places where different rationalities meet and are contested: for example, where state officials implementing a housing megaproject encounter residents of an informal settlement. At the interface, issues may be 'rendered technical' (turned into technical problems that hide political choices), or marginalised groups may win gains. It matters because it is where planning actually happens and where conflicts of rationality become visible. Planners therefore need to understand the rationalities on both sides, including conflicts within the state and within communities, rather than assume a shared public interest. The authors also note that actors can cross the interface, so it is not a simple binary.",
rubric:["Defines interface as meeting place of rationalities","Mentions rendering technical or possibility of gains","Explains relevance for planners (understanding rationalities; not a simple binary)"]},

{t:"p6",fmt:"apply",pts:10,
q:"A hypothetical municipality in a fast-growing African city plans in situ upgrading of an informal settlement: paving roads, adding water standpipes and issuing land titles to 'owners'. Many residents rent rooms from resident landlords. Analyse the plan with lecture 6 and de Satgé and Watson.",
model:"In situ upgrading is the approach lecture 6 favours over resettlement, but the plan raises several issues. First, the lecture warns that upgrading can benefit landlords: better roads and water raise values and rents, so tenants may be displaced while owners gain. Titling sharpens this: critics of De Soto argue that formalisation favours those who can exploit the housing market and that tenure security does not require title; protection from eviction and recognition through services can secure tenants too. Second, conflicting rationalities: the municipality's techno-managerial view (owners, titles, standard infrastructure) may clash with residents' survival logics. As in Langa, the settlement is likely to contain competing groups (owners, tenants, newcomers, long-term residents) whose claims the state's tidy categories miss; de Satgé and Watson found the N2 Gateway's megaproject logic failed to read this granular mix. Third, process: if officials assume they 'know what the people want', participation will be thin. A better plan would start by mapping rationalities and tenure arrangements at the interface, co-produce priorities with residents, protect tenants (rent agreements, tenure security without full titles), and build on residents' own planning and maintenance practices (inverse governmentality, self-production).",
rubric:["Identifies landlord/tenant displacement risk of upgrading","Uses De Soto critique (tenure without title; formalisation favours market-savvy)","Applies conflicting rationalities / heterogeneity within community (Langa/N2 Gateway insight)","Recommends context-first, co-produced approach consistent with the reading"]},

{t:"p6",fmt:"compare",pts:10,
q:"Both Næss (2001) and de Satgé and Watson (2018) reject consensus-based planning. Compare their reasons.",
model:"Næss rejects all-stakeholder consensus from an environmental-goals standpoint in a wealthy Northern context. Sustainability means redistributing consumption from rich people now to others elsewhere and in the future, which will not emerge from dialogue in affluent communities whose lifestyles are part of the problem. A consensus criterion for truth fails for ecological facts, and actors who profit from unsustainability will not join. So he wants goal-oriented planning, scenarios and alliances among those who share equity and environmental values, assuming a capable state that can act on expert knowledge.\nDe Satgé and Watson reject consensus from a Southern, justice standpoint. In cities like Cape Town, state-society relations are shaped by deep conflicting rationalities between a techno-managerial, marketised state and marginalised communities, not by a shared public interest from which Habermasian dialogue could start. Power defines what counts as rational, so expert knowledge is itself suspect. They want planners to understand context and work with deep difference.\nThe contrast: Næss distrusts consensus because it is too weak to impose substantive goals; de Satgé and Watson distrust it because it hides whose rationality defines the goals. They would ask Næss whose sustainability goals his alliances serve.",
rubric:["Næss's reasons (redistribution, knowledge, power; alliances) correctly","De Satgé & Watson's reasons (conflicting rationalities, no public interest, power/rationality) correctly","Notes different contexts (North/capable state vs South)","Identifies the core contrast (too weak vs whose goals)"]}
);

// ===== p7 Planning for sustainability in Chinese cities (Liu & Zhou 2021) =====
OQ_8.push(
{t:"p7",fmt:"essay",pts:25,
q:"(a) Comprehension: explain how the 1994 fiscal reform led to land finance and a land-driven growth coalition. (b) Essay: can the Territorial Spatial Planning system overcome the drivers of unsustainable urbanisation in China? Use Liu and Zhou (2021), lecture 7 and at least one other lecture.",
model:"(a) After the 1994 fiscal reform local governments kept only about a quarter of tax revenue, so land became their main resource: under the dual land system, rural collective land must be expropriated into state ownership before urban use, and urban use rights can then be sold. Local governments sold cheap industrial land to attract investment and expensive residential land to raise revenue. This land finance created a land-driven growth coalition with environmental costs (sprawl, farmland loss) and social costs (expropriation, displacement).\n(b) Liu and Zhou diagnose fragmentation: four main plan types under different ministries, different horizons and land classifications, more than eighty kinds of spatial plans, frequent revisions and no superior law, causing wasted land, ecological degradation and regional imbalance. The reform answers this with one ministry (MNR, 2018), five levels, 'multiple plans in one', a 'One Map', three control lines (ecological red line, permanent basic farmland, urban development boundary) and a planned law that forbids changing plans without legal procedures.\nThis addresses the technical side of fragmentation. But the authors themselves name the root: 'the game of interests between the central and local governments, between different departments'. Lecture 7 shows what drives that game: land finance and the growth coalition. Unless local revenue stops depending on land sales, local governments retain an incentive to push boundaries. Implementation is also uneven: Guangzhou's 2021 to 2035 plan, with 15-minute living circles and ecological corridors, lacked full legal backing until its regulations took effect in late 2025. And participation remains 'authoritarian deliberation', so local knowledge enters late. Liu and Zhou also note that control lines must keep ecosystems connected and that boundaries must plan for shrinkage, with more than 10,000 towns losing population.\nFrom other lectures: Campbell (L3) would read the control lines as settling the resource conflict by decree while leaving property and development conflicts open (who loses land, who gains). Næss (L1) would welcome binding limits on land take. The Dutch case (L4) shows that integration without hard limits leaves 'more claims than land' to negotiation.\nConclusion: the TSP system can curb sprawl and protect farmland and ecology more effectively than before, but it treats a political economy problem mainly as a coordination problem. Lasting change needs fiscal reform and real participation alongside the lines on the map.",
rubric:["(a) Fiscal reform, dual land system/expropriation, cheap industrial vs expensive residential land, growth coalition","Accurate account of fragmentation and NTSP measures (MNR, control lines, One Map, law)","Identifies central-local interests/land finance as unresolved driver; implementation limits (Guangzhou law)","Links at least one other lecture accurately (Campbell, Næss, Dutch system)","Reasoned conclusion"]},

{t:"p7",fmt:"essay",pts:25,
q:"(a) Comprehension: explain 'authoritarian deliberation' using the Enning Road case. (b) Essay: can collaborative planning work outside liberal democracies, and does it work better inside them? Compare China with at least two other contexts from the course.",
model:"(a) Participation in China was legalised (2006, 2008 Act) but remains bounded by the state: authoritarian deliberation. In Enning Road, Guangzhou, a plan for demolition led to protest in 2007; by 2018 the approach had shifted to micro-regeneration with a co-creation committee. Participants could urge the developer or government to act, social conflict fell and there was a sense of moral responsibility. But influence was limited: the committee came only at the implementation stage, and power relations stayed unequal. Participation was instrumental (reducing conflict) rather than normative (empowerment).\n(b) Collaborative planning travelled to China via Chinese scholars in the early 2000s in response to complexity, social conflict, the internet and a rising middle class. Lin et al. (L10) add that in Guangzhou social media supported informal participation and reshaped government-society relations, yet created new power inequalities. So collaborative forms can reduce conflict without shifting power.\nThe Dutch system (L4) is formally more open: polderen involves stakeholders early to build draagvlak, and the Omgevingswet stresses participation. Yet lecture 4 warns of tokenism and unequal power, and Hansen et al. (L13) found participation in European green space planning mostly stuck at information and consultation. The difference with China is one of degree and legal protection, not of kind.\nDe Satgé and Watson (L6) challenge the premise: in South African cities under a democratic government, officials avoided engagement because government 'knows what the people want', and conflicting rationalities run too deep for Habermasian consensus. Næss (L1) doubts consensus even in Norway, because powerful actors gain from unsustainability.\nConclusion: collaborative planning can work outside liberal democracy as a conflict-management tool, as Enning Road shows, but not as empowerment. Inside democracies it has more legal room, yet it also often becomes instrumental. Across contexts, the decisive factors are when participation starts, who holds power and whether outcomes can change plans, not the regime label alone.",
rubric:["(a) Defines authoritarian deliberation; Enning Road from demolition/protest to micro-regeneration; positives and limits (late, unequal, instrumental)","Uses China-specific context (introduction of collaborative planning, Guangzhou social media)","Compares with Dutch polderen/tokenism and/or Hansen participation findings","Uses de Satgé & Watson or Næss to question consensus more broadly","Conclusion identifies decisive factors beyond regime type"]},

{t:"p7",fmt:"essay",pts:25,
q:"(a) Comprehension: explain hukou and the dual land system and how they shape Chinese urbanisation. (b) Essay: are Shenzhen's urban villages a sustainability problem or part of the solution? Compare with the informality debate in African cities.",
model:"(a) Hukou, created in 1958, registers people by birthplace as rural or urban. Urban hukou gives access to urban public services; rural hukou implies a plot of land in the village. After the 1980s reforms, cities needed migrant labour, but rural migrants often still cannot obtain urban hukou and its services. The dual land system splits state-owned urban land (use rights transferable) from rural collective land, which cannot be used for urban purposes until the state expropriates it. Together they create a group of migrants without full urban rights, and villages that are surrounded by the city but remain under collective ownership.\n(b) In Shenzhen, which grew from villages to a metropolis, 2,042 urban villages house around 60% of residents and contain over 40% of the city's building area. The 1996 master plan covered the whole city, but weak management outside the special economic zone gave limited control over these peripheries. Viewed as a problem, they are dense, unplanned and often of low quality, and urban regeneration has meant large-scale demolition and conflict. Viewed as a solution, they provide affordable, central housing for migrants excluded by hukou, at high density close to jobs, which is what compact-city advocates seek.\nThe African debate (L6) sharpens the comparison. Roy treats informality as a mode of urbanisation and a state practice: the state decides what is legitimate. That fits Shenzhen, where the dual land system creates the 'informal' status. De Soto would formalise, but critics note tenure can be secure without title and that formalisation favours those who can exploit markets. Angelo and Wachsmuth (L2) show informal settlements recast as green models, which can romanticise poor conditions. Lecture 6's warning also applies: upgrading or redevelopment can benefit those who hold land or let rooms while tenants are displaced.\nConclusion: urban villages are part of the solution as affordable, dense housing, provided regeneration follows the micro-regeneration route rather than demolition, and that migrant tenants, not only landholders, are protected. The underlying fix is hukou and land reform, not clearance.",
rubric:["(a) Hukou (1958, rural/urban, service access, migrant exclusion) and dual land system (expropriation requirement) correct","Shenzhen urban village facts used accurately","Arguments both for problem and solution","Comparison with African informality debate (Roy, De Soto, upgrading/landlords) accurate","Conclusion with attention to tenants/regeneration approach"]},

{t:"p7",fmt:"short",pts:5,
q:"Explain what Liu and Zhou mean by planning 'dislocation, offside and vacancy' in China's old spatial planning system, with an example.",
model:"The phrase describes the poor coordination of the old system: plans in the wrong place (dislocation), plans overstepping each other's remit (offside), and missing plans where they were needed (vacancy). It resulted from planning being spread over more than ten national departments, each with its own plan type and levels. Example: land-use, urban-rural construction, main functional zone and environmental protection planning were run by different ministries with different planning periods, and urban-rural planning used a three-level land classification while land-use planning used a two-level one, so the same farmland, forest or grassland could carry different attributes and uses in different plans. All of these also rested on the unstable five-year socioeconomic plan.",
rubric:["Explains the three terms as coordination failures","Links to multiple departments/plan types","Gives a correct example (different ministries/periods or land classifications)"]},

{t:"p7",fmt:"apply",pts:10,
q:"A hypothetical Chinese county must draw its urban development boundary and ecological protection red line. Its villages are emptying while the county town wants to expand. Advise the county using Liu and Zhou (2021).",
model:"Liu and Zhou would first require an assessment of resource and environmental carrying capacity and of land suitability as the basis for the plan. The ecological red line should protect areas with key ecological functions (water conservation, biodiversity) and keep them connected, to avoid creating 'ecological islands'. Permanent basic farmland must be protected for food security. The urban development boundary should not assume continuous growth: between 2000 and 2010 more than 10,000 towns and street offices lost population, and village numbers fell from 3.77 million in 1990 to 2.61 million in 2016, so the county should plan for shrinkage as well as growth, avoiding wasted infrastructure in villages likely to disappear. The plan should combine top-down arrangement with bottom-up needs: county plans deal with layout, intensity and environmental quality within national and provincial strategic frames, and villagers' development needs should be included. It should use the 'One Map' data platform so that boundaries align across sectors. Lecture 7 adds a warning: land finance gives the county town an incentive to draw a generous boundary, so third-party evaluation and legal procedures against later changes matter.",
rubric:["Carrying capacity/suitability as basis","Ecological red line connectivity and farmland protection","Plans for shrinkage with correct evidence","Top-down/bottom-up balance and One Map","Mentions incentive problem (land finance) or legal safeguards"]},

{t:"p7",fmt:"compare",pts:10,
q:"Compare Liu and Zhou (2021) with Næss (2001) on how planning should secure sustainability.",
model:"Both are substantive and goal-oriented. Liu and Zhou trust an integrated, top-down system: one ministry, one plan hierarchy, a 'One Map' and binding control lines that limit urban expansion and protect farmland and ecological areas, backed by law and big data. Sustainability is defined from above through 'ecological civilisation'. Næss also wants binding substantive limits: less land take, less energy use, compact development and an end to growth in floor space per person in rich countries, and he distrusts incrementalism and consensus because they cannot deliver such goals.\nThe differences lie in legitimacy and growth. Næss insists on democratic procedures: combining deliberation and voting, transparency through impact analysis and public monitoring, and alliances among supporters of equity and environmental values; he warns against majority tyranny over minorities. Liu and Zhou treat participation mainly as consultation (publishing drafts, hearings) within a state-led system, and lecture 7's 'authoritarian deliberation' shows its limits. On growth, Næss questions continued growth of the stock itself, while Liu and Zhou aim at 'high-quality development', balancing development and protection rather than limiting growth. Næss would welcome the control lines but ask who defines the goals.",
rubric:["Shared substantive/goal-oriented stance with specifics from both","Difference on democratic legitimacy/participation","Difference on growth (per-capita limits vs high-quality development)","Explicit comparison"]}
);

// ===== p8 Urban sustainability transitions (Hodson, Geels & McMeekin 2017) =====
// Lecture slides not available; questions built on the mandatory reading.
OQ_8.push(
{t:"p8",fmt:"essay",pts:25,
q:"(a) Comprehension: explain the Multi-Level Perspective (niche, regime, landscape) and the geographers' critique of it. (b) Essay: why is the realisation of sustainable urban infrastructure so uneven between cities? Use Hodson et al. (2017) and at least two other lectures.",
model:"(a) The MLP sees a transition as a multi-decade shift from one socio-technical system to another. The landscape is the broad external context (demographics, ideologies, climate change, macro-economics), usually slow but sometimes shocked (wars, oil crises). The regime is the set of rules, routines, regulations and standards that structure, but do not determine, actors' behaviour. Niches are protected spaces where radical innovations are nurtured. Classically, landscape pressure cracks the regime and opens a window for a niche innovation. Geographers criticised the MLP for national case studies that treated space as given; for them space and scale are produced relationally, regimes can be territorially uneven, and the same niche looks different in different places, so multiple pathways co-exist.\n(b) Hodson et al. start from this puzzle and give three answers. First, systems correspond differently to the city: automobility is governed by global carmakers, national governments and oil interests, rail is largely national, while buses, light rail and cycling correspond more strongly to the urban scale. Cities can therefore shape some systems far more than others. Second, governance is multi-level: the UK's 1980s bus deregulation shaped local bus systems from above, and the state enrols cities in various modes, sometimes bypassing them. 'Cities are not bounded containers'. Third, innovation lies in reconfiguration: London's bike hire built on a decade of policies (2001 cycling strategy, 2003 congestion charge, bus lanes), so it cannot simply be copied; Oxford promoted electric mobility while Brighton favoured cycling.\nOther lectures confirm this. The Dutch system (L4) shows how responsibilities are split across levels (national rail, municipal zoning and cycling). China (L7) shows how local fiscal incentives (land finance) push cities towards growth rather than sustainable infrastructure, and how fragmentation across ministries hinders integration. Angelo and Wachsmuth (L2) warn against methodological cityism, explaining outcomes only from inside city boundaries.\nConclusion: unevenness is not a failure of individual cities but follows from different system correspondences, multi-level governance and path-dependent local histories. Comparing cities therefore requires looking at how innovations are reconfigured in place, which is exactly Hodson et al.'s proposal.",
rubric:["(a) Landscape, regime, niche correctly defined with classic mechanism; geographical critique (space produced, uneven regimes)","Explains correspondence between systems and city with examples","Explains multi-level governance with UK bus deregulation or state enrolment","Uses reconfiguration/London or Oxford-Brighton","Links at least two other lectures accurately and concludes"]},

{t:"p8",fmt:"essay",pts:25,
q:"(a) Comprehension: describe Banister's four sets of actions for sustainable mobility as used by Hodson et al. (b) Essay: should cities pursue one dominant pathway to sustainable mobility, or embrace multiplicity? Use Hodson et al. and at least two other course authors.",
model:"(a) Hodson et al. list four sets of actions: reducing the need to travel (teleworking, online shopping); modal shift away from the car (slowing traffic, reallocating road space, walking, cycling, trams); reducing trip lengths through urban form (compact, smart or even traffic-free cities); and greater efficiency through new engines, fuels and standards. Each involves several systems and each is contested. Behind them lies a basic disagreement: should reliance on the car be reduced, or can automobility itself be made sustainable?\n(b) Hodson et al. embrace multiplicity. A city hosts many innovations at once, and regimes (auto-, bus- and velomobility) 'compete, coexist and complement'. Sustainability is negotiated between actors, and 'sustainable urban mobility provides orientation and a suite of possibilities rather than a prescribed route map'. Their examples show why: almost 14% of the UK workforce are home workers, reducing travel, but this came from economic restructuring and ICT, not purposeful policy; and the compact city has no accepted definition, with a 'compact city paradox' between density and liveability.\nOther authors push towards choosing. Næss (L1) wants substantive goals: compact, land-saving structures and less car travel, because more roads attract more car commuting. Durrant et al. (L12) argue new infrastructure should be coordinated with phase-outs: expanding cycling and walking at the expense of roads, rail with reduced air capacity. Efficiency alone is suspect because of the Jevons paradox. Campbell (L3) reminds us that choices also have distributional effects (Krumholz's rail versus bus).\nMy view: multiplicity is a good analytical lens, because cities do contain competing pathways and context matters. But as a normative strategy, 'embracing' multiplicity risks letting the efficiency pathway (electric cars) co-exist with continued car dependence, so nothing is phased out. Cities should accept several complementary measures, but with a clear direction: reduce car dependence, and make that explicit in space allocation. Hodson et al. themselves ask which accounts of sustainability become dominant and who promotes them; planners should answer that question deliberately rather than let it emerge.",
rubric:["(a) All four actions correctly with the underlying car-reduction vs sustainable automobility disagreement","Explains Hodson's multiplicity (compete/coexist/complement; orientation not route map) with an example","Uses at least two other authors (Næss, Durrant, Campbell) accurately for a 'choose a direction' position","Distinguishes analytical vs normative use of multiplicity","Clear conclusion"]},

{t:"p8",fmt:"essay",pts:25,
q:"(a) Comprehension: explain the three entry points of Hodson et al.'s framework and the distinction between competing, co-existing and complementary relations. (b) Essay: 'Cities are not bounded containers.' Discuss how national and global forces shape urban sustainability transitions, using Hodson et al. and at least two other lectures.",
model:"(a) The framework looks at the relationship between existing configurations (old) and experimental assemblages (new) through three entry points: socio-technical arrangements (what do all the experiments add up to?), forms of urban governance (how new networks relate to existing arrangements) and conceptions of urban sustainability (which accounts dominate and who promotes them). For each, relations can be competing (struggles of new against new or new against old), co-existing (parallel, largely independent) or complementary (productive fusions). In governance the parallel terms are mutually hostile, loosely coupled or symbiotic.\n(b) Hodson et al. argue that the city is where a transition becomes visible, but global flows (economic restructuring, replicable infrastructures, carbon agreements) and neoliberal restructuring set its conditions, filtered by the institutions that speak for a city. The national state enrols cities in five modes: fiscal and regulatory levers, 'calculative instrumentalism' through emission targets, selectively enabling place-based entrepreneurship, 'marketised individualisation' that bypasses city institutions, and nationally promoted experiments. UK bus deregulation in the 1980s is their example of national decisions shaping local systems.\nOther lectures show the same. Angelo and Wachsmuth (L2) explain the urban turn itself as a product of international development networks and failing climate treaties; mayors signed the Chicago Climate Charter because national policy changed. In China (L7) the central state shapes cities through the planning hierarchy, control lines and fiscal rules (land finance after 1994), and eco-cities combine the eco-state, local entrepreneurialism and policy mobility. In the Netherlands (L4) the decentralised unitary state gives municipalities binding plans within national and provincial frames, and the Omgevingswet allows higher levels to intervene through project decisions; the energy transition in Moerdijk (L5) is a national claim landing on a local place.\nConclusion: urban transitions are multi-scalar. Cities have real agency in systems that correspond to their scale, but national fiscal rules, regulation and global markets shape what is possible. Analysing a city alone (methodological cityism) misses both the constraints and the levers above it.",
rubric:["(a) Three entry points and the competing/co-existing/complementary distinction correct","Explains global flows and state enrolment modes or bus deregulation","Uses at least two other lectures accurately (A&W, China, Dutch system, Moerdijk)","Balances urban agency with external constraints","Conclusion referencing methodological cityism or multi-scalarity"]},

{t:"p8",fmt:"short",pts:5,
q:"Explain why Hodson et al. argue that 'there is nothing particularly novel and innovative about a bike' when discussing London's bike-sharing scheme.",
model:"London's scheme (from 2010, about 8,000 bikes and 500 docking stations by 2014, run by Serco for Transport for London and sponsored by Barclays) used generic components: bikes, smartcards, payment, docking stations and GPS, and learned from Paris's Vélib. What made it work was how it was embedded in a longer local policy trajectory: a 2001 cycling strategy, the 2003 congestion charge and bus lanes open to cyclists. For Hodson et al. the innovation therefore lies in the process of embedding and reconfiguring existing systems in a specific place, not in the technology. That is why the same intervention can be radical in one city and normal in another.",
rubric:["Notes generic technology / learned from Paris","Names embedding in prior policies","States innovation = reconfiguration/process in place"]},

{t:"p8",fmt:"apply",pts:10,
q:"A hypothetical mid-sized Dutch city introduces shared e-bikes and a car-free city centre, while the national government expands a nearby motorway. Analyse this with Hodson et al.'s (2017) concepts.",
model:"Reconfiguration: the innovation is not the e-bike but how the scheme is embedded in existing systems: cycling infrastructure, public transport, parking policy and the car-free centre. Its success will depend on that local trajectory, as London's bike hire did. Multiplicity: several regimes co-exist in the region. Velomobility and automobility may compete (for road space and users), co-exist (cycling inside the centre, car commuting on the motorway) or complement each other (park-and-bike at the edge). The motorway expansion suggests that at the regional scale they co-exist rather than that cycling replaces the car. Correspondence and multi-level governance: cycling and the centre correspond strongly to the municipal scale, while the motorway is national; the city cannot control the system that most shapes regional car use. Competing conceptions of sustainability: the city pursues modal shift and liveability; the national expansion may be justified by congestion relief or efficiency. Hodson et al. would ask which account dominates, who promotes it, and what the experiments add up to across the urban region, rather than judging the e-bike scheme in isolation.",
rubric:["Applies reconfiguration (embedding, not technology)","Applies competing/co-existing/complementary regimes","Applies correspondence/multi-level governance (municipal vs national)","Applies competing conceptions of sustainability"]},

{t:"p8",fmt:"compare",pts:10,
q:"Compare how Hodson et al. (2017) and Næss (2001) treat the meaning of 'sustainability'.",
model:"Hodson et al. treat sustainability as plural and contested. Competing conceptions (air quality, carbon, congestion, local economy; reducing the need to travel versus efficiency) shape which experiments are pursued, and since the 2007 to 2008 crisis 'urban sustainability' has jostled with smart, eco-, green and resilient city labels. Their task is analytical: map which accounts become dominant, who promotes them and how they compete, co-exist or reinforce each other. They do not choose one.\nNæss treats sustainability substantively. Based on Brundtland, it means meeting everyone's basic needs within ecological limits, with distributive ethics across generations and space. He turns this into criteria (lower energy use and emissions per person, less land conversion, closed loops), and warns that stretching the concept to cover everything waters it down, a way for dominant discourses to de-radicalise a challenger.\nSo Hodson et al. study whatever sustainabilities actors mobilise, while Næss would object that leaving the concept this open lets unsustainable practices claim the label. Hodson et al. might reply that Næss's single definition overlooks how meanings are negotiated in specific places.",
rubric:["Hodson: plural, contested, analytical mapping of conceptions","Næss: substantive Brundtland-based criteria; warns against watering down","Identifies the core contrast (map vs prescribe)","Possible replies of each side"]}
);

// ===== p9 Urban experimentation and co-creation (Bulkeley et al. 2019) =====
// Lecture slides not available; questions built on the mandatory reading.
OQ_8.push(
{t:"p9",fmt:"essay",pts:25,
q:"(a) Comprehension: explain the difference between the 'demonstration' and 'platform' dispositions of urban living labs, with an example of each. (b) Essay: who governs the experimental city? Discuss who leads and who benefits from urban living labs, using Bulkeley et al. (2019) and at least two other lectures.",
model:"(a) Both are dispositions where contingency outweighs control. A demonstration is a showcase of what the city could look like under the right conditions; power works through 'government', shaping the conduct of conduct, establishing potential without determining its use. Examples: Aspern Urban Lakeside in Vienna (strategic demonstration) and Concept House Village in Rotterdam (civic demonstration). A platform is the most contingent: it does not fix relations but creates conditions under which many new relations form, curating connectivity between previously disconnected actors; MK: Smart in Milton Keynes (civic platform) and Stapeln in Malmö (organic platform).\n(b) Bulkeley et al.'s design types answer the 'who leads' question. Strategic labs are steered by national or regional government and large corporations, treating the city as a test-bed for national and corporate priorities (E.ON's Hållbarheten in Malmö). Civic labs centre on municipal priorities with universities and local firms. Organic labs are led by civil society around community needs, with small budgets and volunteer time. The authors stress that improvement goals 'can seek to reinforce the status quo', so who sets priorities matters. Leadership is also tactical: in MK: Smart the lead shifted to the Open University under austerity, and Concept House Village existed only because the crisis had stalled redevelopment ('You got it for five years'). They call for research on whether labs can think beyond the interests of the most powerful actors.\nOther lectures deepen this. Hansen et al. (L13) find participation in green space planning mostly at information and consultation, a warning that ULLs' 'user involvement' can be shallow. De Satgé and Watson (L6) remind us that who defines a problem as technical is an exercise of power. Lin et al. (L10) show digital co-design (Zwolle, Eindhoven) can include marginalised groups but also that tools carry bias. China's top-down experiments (L7) resemble strategic demonstrations led by the state.\nConclusion: the experimental city is governed mainly by coalitions of municipalities, universities and firms, with civil society leading only in resource-poor organic labs. Benefits flow to those who set the problem. Experimentation becomes more just when communities share in defining problems, not only in testing solutions.",
rubric:["(a) Correct contrast of demonstration vs platform (contingency, conduct of conduct, connectivity) with correct examples","Uses strategic/civic/organic types to analyse leadership","Uses case evidence on contingency/power (MK: Smart austerity, Concept House 'five years', status quo)","Links at least two other lectures accurately","Conclusion on who benefits"]},

{t:"p9",fmt:"essay",pts:25,
q:"(a) Comprehension: what makes something a 'laboratory' according to Bulkeley et al., and why do they draw on Dewey's notion of inquiry? (b) Essay: compare urban living labs in Europe with sustainability experiments in Chinese cities (eco-cities, smart cities, greenways). Are they the same kind of experimentation?",
model:"(a) Laboratories combine intervention with observation in an enhanced environment where phenomena are manipulated towards desired characteristics ('improvement'). They also have a 'disposition' towards control and contingency: flexible enough for reconfiguration, controlled enough to hold together. Following Dewey, experimentation is 'inquiry': a situation is problematised as undetermined and then given a determination and solution. This makes ULLs political: they are part of deciding which problems need solving and how.\n(b) Similarities: both are responses to uncertainty about what a sustainable city is, and both often take the form of demonstrations. China's eco-cities (more than 259) such as the Sino-Singapore Tianjin Eco-City, planned in 2008 on 30 km2 with water-saving, solar and green-building technologies, resemble Bulkeley et al.'s strategic demonstrations such as Aspern: state and corporate partners showcase technologies on a bounded site. Wu (2015, on the L7 slides) describes eco-cities as combining the eco-state, local entrepreneurialism and policy mobility, which echoes strategic labs' mix of national priorities and first-mover advantage.\nDifferences: Chinese experiments are top-down and large-scale (over 900 smart cities, 20,000 km of greenways in Guangdong), and lecture 7 criticises smart cities for scale over effectiveness and hardware over software. European ULLs in the sample lean towards civic and organic forms with municipalities, universities and civil society, smaller budgets and more contingency; trials are rare. Participation differs: in China it takes the form of authoritarian deliberation, in ULLs it is a defining feature, though often shallow.\nShared weakness: lecture 7 asks whether Chinese experiments remain isolated showcases that leave the planning system untouched; Bulkeley et al. conclude that the transformative potential of demonstrations and platforms 'seems to be much more modest than the claims made'. Hodson et al. (L8) add that experiments are often episodic with little strategic connection.\nConclusion: they are related forms of experimentation, differing mainly in who leads and at what scale. Both face the same problem of moving from showcase to system.",
rubric:["(a) Improvement (intervention + observation), disposition (control vs contingency) and Dewey's inquiry with its political implication","Accurate L7 facts on eco-cities/smart cities/greenways","Maps Chinese experiments onto Bulkeley's types/dispositions with justification","Notes differences (leadership, scale, participation)","Shared limit (isolated showcases / modest transformative potential) and conclusion"]},

{t:"p9",fmt:"essay",pts:25,
q:"(a) Comprehension: what do Bulkeley et al. mean by the 'politics of improvement', and why do they reject the idea that living labs are necessarily radical? (b) Essay: using Concept House Village in Heijplaat, discuss what temporary experiments can contribute to sustainable planning in Dutch cities with scarce land. Draw on at least two other lectures.",
model:"(a) Karvonen and van Heur see labs as intentionally radical. Bulkeley et al. disagree: improvement goals 'are not necessarily radical in nature and indeed can seek to reinforce the status quo'. The 'politics of improvement' therefore asks how priorities are set, which techniques of change dominate and how the lab is financed, because these decide whose idea of a better city is tested.\n(b) Concept House Village was started in 2011 by Rotterdam and Delft universities in Heijplaat as a test bed for sustainable building and retrofit. It got the site because the economic crisis had stalled redevelopment; once the market recovered, the municipality moved the area back into mainstream development: 'You got it for five years'. It is a civic demonstration and shows that ULLs often achieve different things than planned.\nWhat temporary experiments contribute: they use land that is temporarily idle, let new building and retrofit techniques be shown in real conditions, and build coalitions between universities and municipalities, the civic demonstration that lets a city 'lead by example'. What limits them: in a tight land market they last only until land becomes valuable again. Dutch active land policy (L4) works in rising markets, so experiments are tolerated in downturns and pushed out in upturns. Witte et al. (L4) show 'more claims than land' in Rotterdam's port region, and Heijplaat itself is a 'fight' village in the port (L5), so experimental space competes with housing, port and energy claims. Hodson et al. (L8) would ask how such an experiment connects to the wider system; Bulkeley et al. note demonstrations offer a 'pre-figurative display' with little attention to scaling up.\nConclusion: temporary experiments are valuable for learning and coalition-building, but their dependence on market downturns makes them fragile. If cities want lasting results, they need to anchor learning in regular instruments (Omgevingsplan rules, building standards, land allocation for experimental uses) rather than rely on leftover space. Bulkeley et al. call for new thinking about such 'failure', which this case illustrates.",
rubric:["(a) Politics of improvement (priorities, techniques, finance) and the status quo argument","Accurate Concept House Village facts (2011, universities, crisis, 'five years', civic demonstration)","Links to Dutch land policy/more claims than land/Heijplaat as port village","Uses Hodson or scaling critique","Conclusion on anchoring learning"]},

{t:"p9",fmt:"short",pts:5,
q:"Explain the 'enclave' disposition of urban living labs and what Bulkeley et al. mean by calling its boundary an 'obligatory passage point'.",
model:"The enclave is a controlled disposition that allows some contingency. It draws a spatial boundary around an experimental neighbourhood or district, treated as a microcosm of the whole city yet set apart from it, so experiments can run with some protection from everyday urban conditions. Because of this boundary, any lesson learned inside must cross it before it can be replicated elsewhere: the boundary becomes an 'obligatory passage point'. This highlights the scaling problem: what works inside a protected enclave may not survive outside it. Organic labs in the sample often took this form.",
rubric:["Defines enclave as bounded microcosm set apart","Places it between control and contingency","Explains obligatory passage point and the scaling implication"]},

{t:"p9",fmt:"apply",pts:10,
q:"In a hypothetical Dutch city, a neighbourhood energy cooperative, the municipality and a university set up a project to test home batteries and shared solar panels in one street, with residents' meetings to collect ideas. Classify it with Bulkeley et al.'s typology and predict its likely strengths and limits.",
model:"Design type: the cooperative suggests organic, but with the municipality and university as partners and likely municipal co-funding it is closest to a civic ULL (municipal priorities, civic university, local partners); if the cooperative leads and funds it with volunteer time it leans organic. The type matters because it determines whose priorities count. Disposition: a single street with a bounded set of households looks like an enclave (a microcosm set apart, lessons must cross the boundary to be replicated) or a demonstration if the aim is to showcase what an energy-neutral street could look like. It is not a trial: conditions are not tightly controlled. Strengths: real-life learning, user involvement, legitimacy for the municipality, and a coalition that can apply for further funding, as civic labs typically do. Limits: Bulkeley et al. find that demonstrations and enclaves rarely scale; their transformative potential is 'more modest than the claims made'. Funding may be one-off, participants may be the already engaged, and the municipality could withdraw support when priorities change, as in Concept House Village. To strengthen it, the project should plan from the start how results enter regular policy and who beyond the street benefits.",
rubric:["Justified classification of design type (civic vs organic)","Justified classification of disposition (enclave/demonstration, not trial)","Strengths consistent with the reading","Limits consistent with the reading (scaling, funding, contingency)"]},

{t:"p9",fmt:"compare",pts:10,
q:"Compare how Hodson et al. (2017) and Bulkeley et al. (2019) approach urban experimentation.",
model:"Hodson et al. deliberately set aside purposive 'laboratory' experiments and focus on implicit governance and socio-technical experiments: uncertain attempts to reconfigure infrastructures in place, often not labelled as experiments (London's bike hire, home working). Their unit is the city as a site of multiple innovations and regimes, and their framework asks what all experiments add up to across socio-technical arrangements, governance and conceptions of sustainability.\nBulkeley et al. study exactly the explicit, purposive form that Hodson et al. leave out: urban living labs with a learning function, participation and evaluation. Their unit is the lab, classified by design (strategic, civic, organic) and disposition (trial, enclave, demonstration, platform), drawing on science studies, Dewey's pragmatism and Foucauldian governmentality.\nThey reach a similar worry. Hodson et al. find many experiments episodic, with little strategic connection, and expect messy reconfiguration rather than clean transitions. Bulkeley et al. find ULLs lean towards demonstrations and platforms whose transformative potential is modest and whose link to transition pathways is ambiguous. Both see experimentation as shaped by fragmented governing capacity and austerity, and both call for comparative empirical work.",
rubric:["Hodson: implicit experiments, reconfiguration, what experiments add up to","Bulkeley: explicit ULLs, typology and theoretical roots","Shared concern about adding up / modest transformative potential","Explicit comparison"]}
);

// ===== p10 Digital planning for sustainable urban futures (Lin, Geertman, Witte & Pinto 2025) =====
// Lecture slides not available; questions built on the mandatory reading.
OQ_8.push(
{t:"p10",fmt:"essay",pts:25,
q:"(a) Comprehension: give Lin et al.'s definition of digital planning and explain its three aims (efficiency, effectiveness, inclusivity). (b) Essay: can digital planning make urban planning more inclusive? Use Lin et al. (2025) and at least two other lectures.",
model:"(a) Lin et al. define digital planning as 'the application of digital technologies and data-driven approaches to enhance efficiency, effectiveness, and inclusivity in planning processes at different scales to improve social, economic, and environmental outcomes for achieving a more sustainable urban future'. Efficiency means efficient resource use and faster, better-coordinated decisions, including real-time monitoring. Effectiveness means better strategies and policies based on analytics and prediction, and wider support through participation. Inclusivity means participation, engagement and empowerment, including marginalised groups in formal and informal channels.\n(b) The editorial's cases show potential. In Zwolle a mapping tool was co-designed with people with disabilities to plan accessible public space; in Finland planners valued a PPGIS tool that could improve data representativeness and equality; in Eindhoven immersive VR let citizens co-design healthier spaces; in Guangzhou social media supported informal participation and reshaped government-society relations.\nBut the same cases reveal limits. Zwolle showed that inclusion depends on hardware and setting, not just software (digital, physical, social and procedural accessibility). Guangzhou produced new power inequalities within and beyond online networks. The editorial lists a digital divide, user-unfriendly tools, socio-political bias, and a skills gap in authorities. Li and Dang (L11) go further: fast, automated 'optimal' solutions can short-circuit deliberation and exclude voices that cannot be quantified. Hansen et al. (L13) show that even without digital tools, participation mostly stays at information and consultation, so a tool alone will not shift power. De Satgé and Watson (L6) add that language and identity filter who can take part, which applies online as well.\nThere is also a goal conflict: efficiency and effectiveness can favour speed and growth, while inclusivity costs time, echoing Campbell's (L3) conflicts.\nConclusion: digital planning can widen participation when tools are co-designed with the groups concerned and embedded in processes that can actually change decisions. Without that, it reproduces existing inequalities in a new medium. Lin et al. themselves insist digitalisation is 'not just the straightforward implementation and use of new technologies'.",
rubric:["(a) Definition close to the text; three aims explained","Gives at least two supporting cases accurately (Zwolle, Finland PPGIS, Eindhoven, Guangzhou)","Gives limits from the editorial (digital divide, bias, power inequalities, skills)","Links at least two other lectures (Li & Dang, Hansen, de Satgé & Watson, Campbell)","Conclusion with conditions for inclusion"]},

{t:"p10",fmt:"essay",pts:25,
q:"(a) Comprehension: distinguish planning support systems (PSS), Planning Support Science and digital planning. (b) Essay: 'Digitalization of planning is not just the straightforward implementation and use of new technologies' (Lin et al. 2025). Discuss this claim with examples from at least two lectures.",
model:"(a) PSS, defined by Britton Harris in 1989, are computer-based tools that help planners and stakeholders with scenario building, forecasting, participation and trend analysis, evolving from GIS and land-use models towards digital twins, AI and VR. Planning Support Science (Geertman and Stillwell) is the scientific study of how such instruments can be fitted to the governance and application in a specific context, echoing Klosterman: planning support should start from the planning activity, not the technology. Digital planning asks how instruments contribute to inclusive and effective planning in practice; it is application-oriented and results-focused. One is a prerequisite for the next.\n(b) The editorial gives several reasons why technology alone is not enough. Supply and demand often do not match, and tools are user-unfriendly; in Eindhoven participants were positive about VR but asked for better visuals and multi-user functions. Organisational culture matters: Nummi et al. show dominant planning styles and institutions can block innovation, and in Finland a technology-driven culture dominated national development. Politics matters: in Sydney political factors shaped every part of urban analytics. Skills are lacking in authorities and universities. Smart city concepts and platforms (Songdo, business-driven ecosystems; Uber, Airbnb) often develop without planning intervention, so data governance is critical.\nOther lectures confirm this. China's smart city movement (L7) grew to over 900 cities but was criticised for scale over effectiveness, hardware over software, replication of model cities and cameras and big screens rather than data quality. Li and Dang (L11) argue that AI is not just a tool but disrupts the profession's core values, deskilling planners and blurring accountability. Bulkeley et al. (L9) show that experiments rarely scale, the same problem Lin et al. raise for co-design.\nConclusion: the claim is well supported. Digitalisation succeeds when it starts from planning tasks and governance (Planning Support Science), is co-developed with users, and is accompanied by institutional reform, training and data standards. Otherwise it repeats the smart city's mistakes: impressive hardware, little effect on planning outcomes.",
rubric:["(a) Correct three-way distinction with Harris/Geertman/Klosterman elements","Uses at least three editorial reasons (mismatch, usability, culture, politics, skills, platforms)","Uses China smart city critique or another lecture accurately","Uses Li & Dang or Bulkeley for a further angle","Conclusion with conditions"]},

{t:"p10",fmt:"essay",pts:25,
q:"(a) Comprehension: how does digital planning differ from the smart city and platform urbanism, according to Lin et al.? (b) Essay: are digital planning tools neutral? Discuss using Lin et al. and at least two other course authors.",
model:"(a) The smart city debate started academically with Giffinger et al.'s six domains but was driven mainly by business: technology firms built whole ecosystems (Songdo), and the concept was long fuzzy and criticised as technocratic with a weak link to citizens' problems. Platform urbanism (Barns) is urbanisation shaped by platforms such as Uber, Airbnb and Alibaba's City Brain. Digital planning may use both as tools, but it is strategic, procedural and operational, more government-led and consciously guided by policy goals of sustainable and inclusive development.\n(b) Lin et al. explicitly warn of socio-political bias: tools can reshape power relations and create new inequality and exclusion. Their cases show this. In Sydney political factors shaped every aspect of urban analytics, from data availability to use. In Guangzhou social media empowered citizens but created new power inequalities. Batty and Yang's model keeps a democratic decision-making loop next to the evidence loop, precisely because data do not decide by themselves.\nLi and Dang (L11) sharpen the point: AI is sold as impartial, but data carry historical biases (redlining) and ignore unquantifiable values, and once validated, algorithms gain authority over planners' judgement. De Satgé and Watson (L6) give the theoretical basis: power defines what counts as rational, and framing knowledge as 'technical' is itself an exercise of power. Campbell (L3) suggests that efficiency and effectiveness can serve the growth corner while inclusivity serves equity, so tools embed priorities.\nA counterpoint: tools can also counter bias. Zwolle's co-designed mapping tool gave people with disabilities a voice, and PPGIS can improve the representativeness of data. Neutrality is therefore not a property of the tool but of its design and use.\nConclusion: digital tools are not neutral. They embody choices about data, categories and users, and they shift power to those who control them. Planners should treat them as political instruments: co-design them, make their assumptions transparent, and keep human, democratic judgement in the loop.",
rubric:["(a) Smart city (business-driven, fuzzy) and platform urbanism defined; digital planning's distinguishing features","Uses Lin et al.'s bias warning with cases (Sydney, Guangzhou) or Batty & Yang","Uses at least two other authors (Li & Dang, de Satgé & Watson, Campbell) accurately","Includes counterpoint (tools can include; neutrality depends on design/use)","Clear conclusion"]},

{t:"p10",fmt:"short",pts:5,
q:"Explain Batty and Yang's two-loop model of digital planning as cited by Lin et al. (2025), and why it matters.",
model:"Batty and Yang propose a cyclic planning system with two loops. The evidence analytics loop is driven by data: collecting and analysing data, modelling and predicting. The democratic decision-making loop consists of argument, discussion, design and politics. The model matters because it shows that digital planning is not only about better data: analytics informs, but decisions remain a matter of democratic deliberation. It guards against reducing planning to technical optimisation, the risk Li and Dang see in AI, and fits Lin et al.'s point that digitalisation requires new ways of planning, not just new tools.",
rubric:["Names and describes the evidence analytics loop","Names and describes the democratic decision-making loop","Explains significance (data informs, democracy decides)"]},

{t:"p10",fmt:"apply",pts:10,
q:"A hypothetical Dutch municipality plans to build a digital twin to simulate densification scenarios and invites residents to comment through an online platform. Using Lin et al. (2025), advise the municipality on opportunities, risks and how to proceed.",
model:"Opportunities: Lin et al. note that digital twins combine geospatial, remote sensing and IoT data with AI to build scenarios that support consensus-building, and online platforms can widen engagement and crowdsource local knowledge. Under the Omgevingswet, the Netherlands already emphasises digital participation. The twin could show effects of densification on green space, heat or mobility in an understandable way. Risks: a mismatch between what the technology offers and what planners need; user-unfriendly interfaces; a digital divide that excludes older or less digitally skilled residents; socio-political bias in which data and scenarios are included; a skills gap in the municipality; and cybersecurity. Online comments may also over-represent already active groups, and new power inequalities can arise in online networks (Guangzhou). How to proceed: co-develop the twin with planners, residents and technical experts; start from the planning questions, not the technology (Planning Support Science); combine online and offline channels and make tools accessible in hardware and setting, as the Zwolle case advises; make assumptions and data transparent; train officials; and keep the democratic loop in which choices are argued and decided, so the twin informs rather than replaces deliberation.",
rubric:["Identifies opportunities consistent with the editorial (scenarios, engagement, Omgevingswet)","Identifies at least three risks from the editorial","Recommends co-development/starting from planning task","Recommends inclusion measures (offline, accessibility) and keeping democratic decision-making"]},

{t:"p10",fmt:"compare",pts:10,
q:"Compare Lin et al. (2025) and Li and Dang (2025) on the role of AI in planning.",
model:"Lin et al. are broadly optimistic and pragmatic. AI is one of many digital tools (with PSS, digital twins, VR, PPGIS) that can make planning more efficient, effective and inclusive if co-designed and well governed. In their final warnings they note that AI is shifting from a supportive tool to an autonomous, 'agentic' decision-maker, raising concerns about democratic representation, ethics, privacy and justice, especially when budget-constrained politicians are tempted to substitute human processes with AI. Their answer is governance: transparent, inclusive, human-centred AI, training and institutional reform, without forgetting low-tech tools.\nLi and Dang are far more sceptical. For them AI is not just another tool: unlike CAD and GIS, it automates critical-thinking tasks, causing deskilling, algorithmic bias, diminished democratic engagement and blurred accountability, which threaten public trust and professional legitimacy. They doubt that 'augmentation' or regulation will hold, citing journalism and manufacturing, where supportive tools moved towards replacement, and agencies' lack of resources for audits. They propose reclaiming interpretive agency, community-in-the-loop, oversight bodies, new education and 'slow planning'.\nBoth want human oversight and better education; they differ on whether good governance can contain the disruption.",
rubric:["Lin et al.: AI as one tool; agentic AI warning; governance response","Li & Dang: disruption, four risks, doubt about augmentation/regulation","Shared points (oversight, education)","Core difference stated explicitly"]}
);

// ===== p11 AI, planning and sustainability (Li & Dang 2025) =====
// Short reading (3-page comment), no slides: 2 essays + 3 shorter questions.
OQ_8.push(
{t:"p11",fmt:"essay",pts:25,
q:"(a) Comprehension: why do Li and Dang argue that 'urban AI' differs from earlier planning technologies such as CAD and GIS? (b) Essay: should planners embrace AI to plan sustainable cities? Use Li and Dang (2025) and at least two other course authors or cases.",
model:"(a) CAD in the 1980s and GIS in the 1990s transformed how planners visualise and analyse data, but left interpretation and negotiation to humans; earlier smart city paradigms mostly added incremental ICT. Urban AI uses machine learning that shapes governance and physical planning more autonomously: 'Rather than just supporting planners, AI stands apart by automating critical thinking tasks that were once exclusive to human professionals.' Large language models extend this to reasoning about wider context and values.\n(b) There are reasons to embrace AI. Li and Dang's examples point to sustainability gains: Singapore optimises traffic signals, Copenhagen forecasts energy use to cut emissions, and Los Angeles explores machine learning for fairer housing allocation. Lin et al. (L10) show digital tools can support scenarios and participation. The authors also argue the disruption is unavoidable: developers, transport firms and politicians push AI, and agencies that do not keep up risk becoming uncompetitive.\nBut the risks are serious. Deskilling: 'optimal' algorithmic outputs gain authority and displace practitioner wisdom. Bias: data reflect historical inequities such as redlining and ignore unquantifiable values. Democratic engagement shrinks when automated solutions bypass slow deliberation. Accountability blurs: if an algorithmic site choice triggers gentrification, who is responsible? A Dutch non-planning case shows these mechanisms: the tax authority's risk model for childcare benefits used nationality as a risk indicator, many parents were wrongly treated as fraudsters, and the cabinet resigned in 2021. For sustainability planning, which Campbell (L3) frames as negotiating conflicts between growth, environment and equity, reducing city-making to 'a purely technical optimization problem' is especially problematic: the trade-offs are value choices, not optimisation problems. De Satgé and Watson (L6) would add that whoever defines the 'technical' frame exercises power.\nConclusion: planners should neither reject nor simply adopt AI. Following Li and Dang, they should set the terms: act as 'sense-makers' who interpret outputs and keep responsibility (like doctors with clinical decision-support), embed communities in data and model building, demand transparency and audits, update education and keep space for 'slow planning'. AI can serve sustainable cities only as a tool under democratic and professional control.",
rubric:["(a) CAD/GIS kept human interpretation; AI automates critical thinking (quote or close paraphrase)","Gives arguments for AI (examples, unavoidability)","Explains at least three of the four risks accurately","Uses at least two other authors/cases accurately (Lin et al., Campbell, de Satgé & Watson, childcare benefits case)","Conclusion reflecting Li & Dang's lessons"]},

{t:"p11",fmt:"essay",pts:25,
q:"(a) Comprehension: explain the 'accountability gap' that Li and Dang associate with AI in planning. (b) Essay: does AI threaten the planner's professional legitimacy? Compare the planner's role in Li and Dang with the roles proposed by at least two other course authors.",
model:"(a) Planners balance technical feasibility, legal compliance and moral responsibility. AI puts distance between a recommendation and its human overseers. The authors' example: if an automated site selection triggers gentrification, who is responsible, the planner, the software developer or the agency? Without transparent design, open data and a clear chain of accountability, errors are hard to trace, which produces a crisis of professional legitimacy.\n(b) Li and Dang see legitimacy resting on judgement: interpreting community values, reconciling conflicts and adapting policy to context. AI threatens this by deskilling, by making 'optimal' outputs authoritative and by bypassing deliberation. Their answer is a redefined role: planners as 'sense-makers' who interpret AI outputs in light of local histories and values and keep responsibility, like doctors using clinical decision-support.\nOther authors locate legitimacy differently. Campbell (L3) sees planners as mediators or advocates in the triangle's conflicts; legitimacy comes from managing conflicts between growth, environment and equity, a task AI cannot do because it requires choosing values. Næss (L1) grounds legitimacy in substantive goals plus knowledge of consequences, combining expert and lay knowledge, and he wants impact analysis and public monitoring for transparency. Durrant et al. (L12) base the planner's role on expertise about medium- to long-term consequences: planners should 'voice uncomfortable truths', not values, because they are employees of elected politicians. De Satgé and Watson (L6) warn that expertise itself can be power when it defines what is rational.\nComparison: Næss and Durrant et al. make expertise the basis of legitimacy; Li and Dang fear that exactly this expertise is being automated away. But their solution converges: planners remain legitimate when they interpret evidence publicly and take responsibility for value choices, which no algorithm can do.\nConclusion: AI threatens legitimacy if planners become operators of systems they cannot explain. It may strengthen it if they become the accountable interpreters of AI output and the guardians of deliberation. Accountability, not technical skill, is the core of the profession's future legitimacy.",
rubric:["(a) Accountability gap with the gentrification example and transparency/chain of responsibility","Li & Dang's view of legitimacy and sense-maker role","At least two other authors' views of the planner's role accurately (Campbell, Næss, Durrant, de Satgé & Watson)","Explicit comparison (expertise automated vs expertise as basis)","Conclusion"]},

{t:"p11",fmt:"short",pts:5,
q:"Explain what Li and Dang mean by 'deskilling' in planning.",
model:"Planners develop judgement by interpreting community values, reconciling conflicting interests and adapting policies to unique contexts. Once validated, algorithms tend to become authorities: if an AI system suggests an 'optimal' zoning mix, human intervention may be sidelined in the name of efficiency. Over time, reliance on algorithmic outputs 'displaces accumulated practitioner wisdom', so planners lose the skills and confidence to make their own judgements. Deskilling matters because those judgements involve values and local knowledge that data cannot capture, and because a deskilled profession cannot check or take responsibility for automated decisions.",
rubric:["Describes the judgement that planners build","Explains algorithmic authority displacing it ('optimal' output example)","States why it matters (values, accountability)"]},

{t:"p11",fmt:"apply",pts:10,
q:"A hypothetical Dutch municipality trains a machine-learning model on ten years of resident complaints to decide which neighbourhoods get new green space first. Analyse the plan with Li and Dang's four disruptive factors.",
model:"Algorithmic bias: complaint data reflect who complains, not who needs green space. Residents with more time, language skills and trust in government complain more, so the model may favour already advantaged areas and reproduce historic inequities, the way Li and Dang warn AI can reinforce redlining. Needs that cannot be quantified, such as cultural or community values, disappear. Deskilling: if the ranking is treated as 'optimal', planners may stop exercising judgement about local context, conflicts and alternatives. Diminishing democratic engagement: an automated ranking can short-circuit deliberation about priorities, leaving residents fewer reasons to engage. Blurred accountability: if the investment pattern later triggers green gentrification or neglects deprived areas, it is unclear who is responsible. Li and Dang's lessons suggest using the model only as input for planners acting as sense-makers, involving residents of under-represented areas in data collection and validation (community-in-the-loop), checking the model for bias with independent auditing, and keeping the final decision in a transparent political process. The link with lecture 3 is relevant: greening priorities are a justice question, not only an optimisation problem.",
rubric:["Applies algorithmic bias to complaint data concretely","Applies deskilling","Applies democratic engagement and accountability","Recommends measures from Li & Dang's lessons"]},

{t:"p11",fmt:"compare",pts:10,
q:"Compare the 'AI only augments planners' view with Li and Dang's response to it.",
model:"The augmentation view holds that AI takes over routine data tasks and frees planners for strategy, facilitation and value judgements; a related view holds that AI can be regulated through transparent algorithms and oversight committees. Lin et al.'s editorial (L10) leans in this direction: good governance and co-design can keep AI human-centred. Li and Dang grant both views some merit but say they understate AI's disruptive force. First, precedent: in journalism and manufacturing, technology introduced as a supportive tool evolved quickly towards partial or full replacement of human roles. Second, incentives: the promise of speed and cost savings can override longer-term social goals, especially for agencies under budget pressure and politicians who want an 'innovative' city. Third, capacity: agencies often lack the resources and skills to perform rigorous algorithmic audits, especially under pressure to innovate. So balanced augmentation is 'precarious'. Rather than relying on it, they want the profession to set the terms actively: reclaim interpretive agency, embed communities, create oversight and certification bodies, redefine education and resist over-reliance through 'slow planning'. The difference is not about whether augmentation is desirable but whether it will happen by itself.",
rubric:["States the augmentation and regulation views","Li & Dang's three reasons (precedent, incentives, capacity)","Their alternative (setting the terms; lessons)","Identifies the core difference"]}
);

// ===== p12 Planning for post-growth cities (Durrant, Lamker & Rydin 2023) =====
// Lecture slides not available; questions built on the mandatory reading.
OQ_8.push(
{t:"p12",fmt:"essay",pts:25,
q:"(a) Comprehension: distinguish degrowth from post-growth as Durrant et al. (2023) use the terms. (b) Essay: can housing policy be re-tooled for a post-growth city? Use Durrant et al. and at least two other lectures, with attention to the Dutch context.",
model:"(a) Degrowth is more rooted in economics, more structural and theoretical, and gives voice to North-South inequality and different development pathways. Post-growth is an interdisciplinary, pragmatic search for radical alternatives within existing structures; it suits an analysis of today's planning tools while still aiming to transform planning. Both share the empirical core of decoupling.\n(b) Durrant et al. want to 'delink planning policy and practice from the reliance on growth and ever-continuing new urban development to achieve public goals'. For housing they reject the idea, 'for which there is no foundation', that affordability can be solved by more market-led building in high-price areas. Their tools: diversify tenures and use site allocations to protect land for public, social and community housing; ambitious targets for self-build and community-built housing; life-cycle and carbon accounting for every proposal with attention to cumulative impacts; minimum and maximum space standards; ceilings on development redistributed from high-price to high-need areas; planning for shrinkage; courageous reuse of the existing stock; and restricting second homes and short-term lets. They exclude land value capture because it depends on creating more monetised development value.\nNæss (L1) gave the substantive argument two decades earlier: floor space per person in Norway and Denmark doubled in 30 to 35 years, so efficiency gains are cancelled out, and per-capita building stock must stop growing in wealthy countries. Maximum space standards and reuse are his 'selective standard improvement' in tool form.\nThe Dutch context shows both openings and obstacles. Amsterdam limits holiday rental of whole homes to 30 nights a year, a real version of restricting short-term lets. But Dutch active land policy (L4) finances public goals through land sales and only works in rising markets, exactly the growth dependence Durrant et al. criticise; Witte et al. (L4) show 'more claims than land' where housing competes with port, energy and climate claims. Hansen et al. (L13) show greening partly funded by densification (Aarhus's exchange agreements).\nConclusion: re-tooling is possible because many instruments exist (site allocation, space standards, tenure rules); the challenge is to stop treating housing numbers and land revenue as the measure of success. Durrant et al. admit this needs supportive national frameworks and devolved control over land and finance, so it is a political as much as a technical shift.",
rubric:["(a) Degrowth (structural, economic, North-South) vs post-growth (pragmatic, within structures) correct","Accurately lists several housing tools and the land value capture exclusion","Links Næss's per-capita floor space argument","Uses Dutch context accurately (short-term let cap, active land policy, more claims than land)","Conclusion on political/framework conditions"]},

{t:"p12",fmt:"essay",pts:25,
q:"(a) Comprehension: what do Durrant et al. mean by planning for phase-out and by interrogating 'do nothing' scenarios in infrastructure planning? (b) Essay: should new sustainable infrastructure always be coupled with the phase-out of unsustainable infrastructure? Use at least three course authors or cases.",
model:"(a) Durrant et al. argue that new infrastructure should be coordinated with the planned replacement or shrinkage of existing infrastructure: renewable energy investment with phasing out carbon-based infrastructure, cycling and walking networks that take space from roads and car-based spaces, long-distance rail coordinated with reduced air travel capacity. 'Do nothing' scenarios are the baselines used to justify new projects (for example, forecasts of ever-growing traffic); planners should scrutinise them critically rather than accept growth as the default. They also note that existing networks embody sunk resources and capital, so working within them can be better than expanding indefinitely.\n(b) The case for coupling rests on decoupling evidence. Durrant et al. find little evidence of absolute decoupling: rebound (the Jevons paradox) and problem shifting mean additions are rarely substitutions. Hodson et al. (L8) show that in cities new and old regimes usually 'compete, coexist and complement'; London's bike hire co-exists with car traffic. Without phase-out, the new system is added on top of the old one.\nThere are real examples of coupling. Witte et al. (L4) report that Amsterdam allowed no new fuel terminals from 2008, closed a coal terminal in 2023 by not renewing its leasehold and aims to phase out fossil fuel handling by 2030, freeing about 370 ha for hydrogen, electricity and circular industries; Rotterdam, with more space, follows an 'and-and' approach, adding new energy activities next to a modernised petrochemical cluster. Hansen et al. (L13) call for 'exnovation', deliberately dismantling lock-in practices, but find it rare.\nArguments against making it a rule: phase-out has social costs, as Moerdijk (L5) shows when energy infrastructure displaces a village, and as Campbell's (L3) development conflict warns, burdens may fall on workers and residents. Durrant et al. themselves stress decisions will be delicate and context-specific.\nConclusion: coupling should be the default rather than an absolute rule. Every new sustainable project should state what it replaces, with phase-outs planned and their social consequences compensated; otherwise 'sustainable' infrastructure simply adds capacity, the pattern Durrant et al. call growth-dependence.",
rubric:["(a) Phase-out coupling with examples; critical scrutiny of 'do nothing' baselines; sunk resources point","Uses decoupling/Jevons argument","Uses at least two other cases/authors accurately (Hodson, Amsterdam fossil phase-out vs Rotterdam and-and, Hansen exnovation, Moerdijk)","Considers social costs/justice of phase-out","Clear, nuanced conclusion"]},

{t:"p12",fmt:"essay",pts:25,
q:"(a) Comprehension: how do Durrant et al. see the role of the planner in post-growth planning? (b) Essay: can individual planners drive a shift beyond growth, or do structures decide? Discuss with Durrant et al. and at least two other course authors.",
model:"(a) Durrant et al. want planners to move from neutral managers of market-led development to 'supportive holders of shared leadership'. Because planners are mostly employees under elected politicians, they ground the role in expertise rather than values: planners know more than ever about resources and impacts, so they should spell out the medium- to long-term environmental and social consequences of plans and the infeasibility of plans that presuppose growth. 'Planners should consider part of their role to voice uncomfortable truths and to avoid the easier path or discourse of growth-dependence.' They may also support community initiatives and re-tool towards community building.\n(b) The structural constraints are strong. Durrant et al. themselves say planning 'has bound itself into a pro-growth agenda that is mentally and institutionally hard to overcome', with success measured in money, and they leave structural reform to the wider post-growth debate. Hansen et al. (L13) find municipal officials who see what is needed but lack resources and staff, with path dependencies and silos limiting change; transformation happened mainly where high-level policy changed. Liu and Zhou (L7) show how fiscal structures (land finance) drive local growth regardless of plans. Li and Dang (L11) warn that planners' judgement itself may be displaced by AI.\nYet agency is not absent. Næss (L1) argued that planners can assess alternatives against sustainability criteria, develop sustainable alternatives and scenarios (NAMIT probably contributed to less sprawl in Norway) and use official goals to legitimise this, though he warns that planners who expose powerful groups risk being called disloyal and therefore wants legislation requiring impact analysis. Campbell (L3) says planners have no natural home on the triangle and must choose to mediate or advocate.\nConclusion: individual planners cannot drive the shift alone, but they are not powerless. Their leverage lies in making consequences visible (carbon accounting, cumulative impacts, critique of do-nothing scenarios) so that political choices are made knowingly. Structures decide the pace; planners can shift the terms of debate, especially when national frameworks and civil society alliances support them.",
rubric:["(a) Expertise-based role, uncomfortable truths, shared leadership, employees under politicians","Structural constraints with evidence from Durrant and at least one other lecture (Hansen, China, Li & Dang)","Agency arguments with Næss and/or Campbell accurately","Balanced structure-agency conclusion"]},

{t:"p12",fmt:"short",pts:5,
q:"Why do Durrant et al. (2023) exclude land value capture from post-growth planning tools?",
model:"Land value capture funds public goals (affordable housing, infrastructure, green space) by capturing part of the increase in land value that development or planning permission creates. Durrant et al. exclude it because it depends on creating more monetised development value, often in expensive locations, which makes it inherently pro-growth: public goods can only be delivered if more and more lucrative development happens. That ties social and environmental goals to the very process post-growth planning wants to slow. Instead, they propose protecting land for public, social and community housing through site allocations and making space for low-value and non-commercial uses.",
rubric:["Explains what land value capture does","States it depends on creating more monetised development value","Explains why that is pro-growth / ties goals to growth"]},

{t:"p12",fmt:"apply",pts:10,
q:"Hypothetically, a fast-growing Dutch city wants 20,000 new homes, while a shrinking municipality elsewhere has empty dwellings and underused facilities. How would Durrant et al. (2023) approach housing in both places?",
model:"Durrant et al. would reject the assumption that more market-led building in the high-price city will solve affordability. In the growing city they would protect land for public, social and community housing through site allocations, set targets for self-build and community-built homes, require life-cycle and carbon accounting for each proposal and look at cumulative impacts, introduce minimum and maximum space standards, require reuse of existing buildings where the carbon analysis supports it, and restrict second homes and short-term lets (Amsterdam's 30-night cap on holiday rental is a real example). They would also set a ceiling on development rather than only a target. At the national level they would favour redistributing development from high-price, high-demand areas to lower-price, high-need areas and abandon competition between growth engines. For the shrinking municipality, plans should consider shrinkage and no or low growth as legitimate scenarios; it could be a testbed for post-growth ideas, but shrinkage is not success in itself and responsibility should not be pushed onto it alone. They add that fast-growing areas may be better placed to lead, and that this all requires supportive national frameworks.",
rubric:["Rejects market-led supply as affordability solution","Applies several housing tools correctly in the growing city","Applies ceilings and redistribution between areas","Treats shrinkage correctly (testbed but not success; not sole responsibility)"]},

{t:"p12",fmt:"compare",pts:10,
q:"Compare Durrant et al. (2023) with Næss (2001) on planning and growth.",
model:"Both reject the idea that efficiency and growth can deliver sustainability on their own. Næss argued that continuous growth of the building stock in rich countries is incompatible with sustainability: floor space per person had doubled in Norway and Denmark, cancelling efficiency gains, so per-capita stock must stop growing. Durrant et al. generalise this with the decoupling debate: there is little evidence of absolute decoupling, because of the Jevons paradox and problem shifting. Both want substantive limits: Næss a national ceiling on resource use and 'selective standard improvement', Durrant et al. development ceilings and maximum space standards.\nThey differ in emphasis and scope. Næss focuses on goals and procedures: he criticises communicative planning and proposes scenarios and alliance-building with supporters of sustainability. Durrant et al. focus on re-tooling existing instruments for housing and infrastructure within existing structures (post-growth, not degrowth). On the planner's role, Næss leans on goal-oriented planning backed by alliances and legislation for impact analysis, while Durrant et al. ground the role in expertise about consequences and 'uncomfortable truths'. Durrant et al. can be read as a toolbox for Næss's diagnosis.",
rubric:["Shared rejection of growth/efficiency-only, with Næss's floor space and Durrant's decoupling","Shared substantive limits (ceiling, standards)","Difference: procedures/alliances vs instrument re-tooling","Difference in planner role"]}
);

// ===== p13 Planning with nature (Hansen et al. 2022) =====
// Lecture slides not available; questions built on the mandatory reading.
OQ_8.push(
{t:"p13",fmt:"essay",pts:25,
q:"(a) Comprehension: describe the four targeted outcomes of urban transformation (Wolfram et al.) that Hansen et al. use as a yardstick. (b) Essay: Hansen et al. studied 'champion' cities and found mostly incremental change. What does this imply for the claim that cities can lead sustainability transformations? Use at least two other lectures.",
model:"(a) Innovation: creating, nurturing and anchoring novelties. Exnovation: exposing and dismantling path dependencies ('unlocking'). Co-governance (originally collective stewardship): enabling and aligning diverse actors across sectors and scales. Social justice: ensuring diversity and contestation and redistributing resources and decision-making power, since technocratic decisions often reinforce inequities. Transformative change is 'intentional, actively pursued, radical and systemic'.\n(b) Hansen et al. revisited eleven European cities, many of them EU research partners and sustainability champions. Green space gained attention: climate change grew in relevance in ten cities, participation efforts increased in nine, cross-departmental cooperation in nine. But the main instruments were unchanged in eight cities, exnovation was rare, participation mostly stayed at information and consultation, and social justice was the weakest lens. Even COVID-19 produced concrete acceleration only in Barcelona. Their verdict: these cities 'cannot be classified as radical urban transformations'. The fundamental constraint is limited resources and staff, caused by system-level conditions beyond green space departments or even municipalities.\nThis tempers the optimism Angelo and Wachsmuth (L2) analyse: the belief that cities can save the planet. If champion cities struggle, ordinary ones will struggle more. It fits Bulkeley et al. (L9), whose living labs mostly produce demonstrations with modest transformative potential, and Hodson et al. (L8), who expect messy reconfiguration and stress that cities are embedded in national frameworks. It also echoes Durrant et al. (L12): planning instruments remain tied to growth (densification funding greenery, as in Aarhus).\nBut incremental is not meaningless. Hansen et al. find deliberate transformative change at high levels of local policy (new policies, co-governance cultures), and incremental changes (systems thinking, integrated planning, citizen interest) may seed future transformations. Helsinki's Green Area Factor shows regulatory change is possible.\nConclusion: cities can initiate and prepare transformations, but they cannot complete them alone. Claims that cities lead should be matched by multi-scale policy frameworks, resources and deliberate exnovation, as Hansen et al. recommend.",
rubric:["(a) All four outcomes correctly defined","Accurate key findings (instruments unchanged, rare exnovation, consultation-level participation, justice weakest, resources)","Links to A&W 'cities save the planet' and at least one more lecture (Bulkeley, Hodson, Durrant)","Acknowledges incremental change as possible seed of transformation","Conclusion on what is needed"]},

{t:"p13",fmt:"essay",pts:25,
q:"(a) Comprehension: what did Hansen et al. find about the relationship between urban growth and green space? (b) Essay: does urban greening depend on growth, and is that a problem? Use Hansen et al. and at least two other lectures.",
model:"(a) Urban growth was ambiguous. Densification brought resources for green space but also conflicts, for example with affordable housing, sharpened by tourism in Berlin and Barcelona; three cities reported tension between greening and densification. Aarhus's Municipal Plan (2017) introduced 'exchange agreements' in which developers contribute to the common good, such as new green spaces, in return for their economic gain. Resources were mixed overall, with budget cuts and maintenance budgets not keeping pace with new green space.\n(b) Greening often does depend on growth. Aarhus's exchange agreements are a form of land value capture: development gains fund green space. Dutch active land policy (L4) has worked similarly, financing public amenities through land sales, but only in rising markets; Utrecht's neighbourhood green plans suffered significant budget cuts. Growth also funds greening indirectly through tax revenue.\nWhy this is a problem: Durrant et al. (L12) exclude land value capture because it depends on creating ever more monetised development value, tying public goals to growth. When growth stalls, greening and especially maintenance suffer, which Hansen et al. identify as a key weakness. Green gentrification (L3) adds a justice problem: green amenities funded through development raise land values, and Gould and Lewis show that rising property values signal 'success' while predicting displacement. Lecture 3's discussion question also notes that compact development to reduce sprawl may leave less green space in the city.\nNot all greening needs growth. Helsinki's Green Area Factor is a regulatory requirement for green space in new developments; Utrecht's guilds and integrated planning reorganise existing resources; co-governance with citizens (Friends of Parks groups, 'Together we make Utrecht') adds capacity, though Hansen et al. warn this rarely reaches deprived areas.\nConclusion: growth-funded greening is a pragmatic short-term route, but a fragile and potentially unjust one. Cities should secure stable maintenance budgets and regulatory standards and target greening to need, with anti-displacement safeguards, so that green space does not depend on, or accelerate, speculative development.",
rubric:["(a) Ambiguous growth relation; Aarhus exchange agreements; maintenance/budget problem","Shows dependence with at least one further example (Dutch land policy, Utrecht budget cuts)","Uses Durrant's land value capture critique and/or green gentrification accurately","Identifies non-growth alternatives (Green Area Factor, integration, co-governance)","Conclusion"]},

{t:"p13",fmt:"essay",pts:25,
q:"(a) Comprehension: explain how Berlin and Utrecht illustrate different paths regarding the integration of urban and green space planning. (b) Essay: is breaking down departmental silos the key to sustainable urban planning? Compare green space planning in Europe with at least one other planning context from the course.",
model:"(a) Berlin reorganised its administration and separated urban planning from green space planning, even physically into different buildings. Cooperation still worked thanks to long-standing working relationships, but the interviewee feared this would erode when staff retire: integration that rests on people rather than structures is fragile. Utrecht moved the other way: it created 'guilds' (gildes) that bring sectors such as urban development and green space planning together in new housing areas, and shifted attention to connectivity and neighbourhood access, especially in lower-income areas. Yet its neighbourhood green plans suffered significant budget cuts.\n(b) Hansen et al. treat silos as a key path dependency. Climate adaptation and sustainable mobility created opportunities for integration; Helsinki and Linz merged units; Aarhus created a sub-department for citizenship and cultural change, the clearest case of exnovation. But integration has costs: in Malmö it increased workloads, and the deeper constraint is resources and staff capacity.\nChina (L7) offers a large-scale comparison. Before 2018, spatial planning was split over more than ten national departments, with four main plan types using different periods and land classifications, causing 'dislocation, offside and vacancy'. The reform merged functions into one ministry (MNR), 'multiple plans in one' and a 'One Map'. Liu and Zhou expect this to break departmental interests, but they also locate the root in a 'game of interests' between central and local governments, which integration alone does not end. The Dutch Omgevingswet (L4) likewise integrates sectoral rules into one act and plan, while the lecture notes multi-level dynamics are not necessarily eased.\nTheory supports integration: Ackoff's 'mess' (L3) means problems cannot be solved separately, and Hodson et al. (L8) show governance arrangements can be hostile, loosely coupled or symbiotic.\nConclusion: breaking silos is necessary but not sufficient. Integration helps only when backed by resources, legal standing and political priorities; otherwise it shifts workloads (Malmö) or depends on individuals (Berlin). Both the European and Chinese cases show that the interests and incentives behind the silos must change too.",
rubric:["(a) Berlin separation and fragility; Utrecht guilds and neighbourhood access, with budget cuts","Uses Hansen findings on integration (Helsinki/Linz, Aarhus, Malmö workloads, resources)","Accurate comparison with China's fragmentation and NTSP integration and/or the Omgevingswet","Uses a conceptual link (Ackoff, Hodson)","Conclusion: necessary but not sufficient"]},

{t:"p13",fmt:"short",pts:5,
q:"Explain 'exnovation' and give an example from Hansen et al. (2022).",
model:"Exnovation is the deliberate exposing and dismantling of path dependencies and lock-in practices ('unlocking'), the counterpart of innovation, which creates and anchors novelties. In planning it means phasing out routines, structures or instruments that block change, such as siloed departments or instruments focused only on recreation and access. Hansen et al. found exnovation rare. The clearest case was Aarhus, which created a sub-department for citizenship and cultural change, a form of change management. Closer cooperation between green space, planning, water and transport units may be a path towards dismantling silos. The authors recommend that political and administrative leaders provide incentives to identify and exnovate lock-in practices.",
rubric:["Correct definition (dismantling path dependencies/lock-in)","Contrasts with innovation or explains relevance","Correct example (Aarhus sub-department) or recommendation"]},

{t:"p13",fmt:"apply",pts:10,
q:"A hypothetical Dutch municipality wants to introduce a mandatory green area requirement for all new developments, like Helsinki's Green Area Factor, but its green maintenance budget has just been cut. Analyse its prospects using Hansen et al. (2022).",
model:"Hansen et al. would see the requirement as deliberate change at the level of formal policy tools, one of the few regulatory rather than voluntary responses in their study (Helsinki adopted its Green Area Factor for climate adaptation, requiring sufficient green space for ecosystem services within new developments). Such a rule anchors innovation in policy and does not depend on public spending for creation, since developers must provide the green. But their findings point to risks. Maintenance budgets have not kept pace with new green space in several cities, and Utrecht's neighbourhood green plans faced significant cuts; more green without maintenance capacity can degrade. The requirement applies only to new development, so it ties greening to growth and does nothing for existing deprived neighbourhoods, where Hansen et al. found few specific actions despite growing awareness of social cohesion. Implementation also needs cooperation across departments (planning, green, water), and silos can block it. Recommendations from the paper: secure long-term maintenance funding, integrate departments (as Utrecht's guilds), add justice criteria so that greening also targets areas of need, and involve residents beyond consultation.",
rubric:["Recognises regulatory/deliberate change (Helsinki example correct)","Identifies maintenance/resource risk with evidence","Identifies growth-dependence and justice gap","Gives recommendations consistent with Hansen et al."]},

{t:"p13",fmt:"compare",pts:10,
q:"Compare Hansen et al. (2022) and Bulkeley et al. (2019) on the transformative potential of current urban sustainability practice.",
model:"Both reach sober conclusions. Bulkeley et al., studying 40 European urban living labs, find that labs lean towards demonstrations and platforms rather than trials, and that their 'transformative potential seems to be much more modest than the claims made', with ambiguous links to transition pathways and little attention to scaling. Hansen et al., studying green space planning in eleven European champion cities, find many changes incremental or ambiguous, so the cities 'cannot be classified as radical urban transformations'.\nThey differ in object and explanation. Bulkeley et al. study purposive experiments outside the regular administration; they explain modest impact through fragmented governing capacity, austerity and the open-ended nature of sustainability. Hansen et al. study the regular municipal administration through officials' eyes; they explain limited change through path dependencies, silos, maintenance and funding, and staff capacity set by system-level conditions. Bulkeley et al. emphasise contingency and who leads (strategic, civic, organic); Hansen et al. use Wolfram's four outcomes and find social justice the weakest. Both see value in small changes: platforms sustain programmes of experiments, and incremental changes may seed future transformation. Together they suggest that neither experiments nor mainstream planning alone transform cities.",
rubric:["Bulkeley: modest transformative potential, demonstrations/platforms","Hansen: mostly incremental, not radical transformation","Different objects and explanations (experiments vs administration; capacity/austerity vs path dependency/resources)","Shared nuance about small changes"]}
);
